  - TypeFullName: Basis.Scripts.Constraints.BasisConstraintSolveJob
    MemberName: BasisConstraintSolveJob
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: IJobParallelFor
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Solves every constraint, walking Order \u2014 a topological order over\n
      the source\u2192target dependency graph, so a constraint driven by another
      constraint's target\n always sees the already-solved pose regardless of where
      either sits in the hierarchy. After\n each slot resolves, the target's entry
      in World is recomposed from its parent,\n which is what makes those chains
      work.\n\n Two caveats worth knowing. Only *constrained* transforms are recomposed:
      an unconstrained\n transform sitting between a constrained parent and a constrained
      child is read at its\n pre-solve world pose for the frame. And a dependency
      cycle has no valid order at all \u2014 it is\n broken at the shallowest member,
      which lags one frame.\n\n One iteration per Groups entry, each a contiguous
      run of Order.\n A group is a connected component of that same dependency graph,
      so no two groups share a\n transform row either of them writes and neither
      can observe the other \u2014 which is what lets\n them run at once without
      giving up the ordering the solve depends on. The sequence inside a\n group
      is exactly what it was, so a room full of avatars simply stops being solved
      one avatar\n at a time. Every write lands through that ordering rather than
      through the iteration index,\n which is what the disabled range checks below
      are for."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
