  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.BasisArmSwivelModel
    MemberName: BasisArmSwivelModel
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 1
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Where the elbow goes for a user with NO elbow tracker -- FITTED to
      real humans, not guessed at.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      THE REFRAME: the elbow is not free, so stop predicting a direction. Predict
      ONE ANGLE.\n\n With the shoulder and the hand both fixed, the elbow is confined
      to a CIRCLE (Korein's swivel angle;\n the standard result for a redundant limb).
      The limb's entire redundancy collapses to ONE SCALAR: the\n swivel about the
      shoulder->hand axis. Predicting the elbow IS predicting that angle.\n\n This
      is THE FIX FOR THE SNAP AT FULL EXTENSION. Any angle you predict lands ON the
      reachable circle by\n construction. A 3-vector \"bend direction\" does not
      -- so the solver needs projections, fades and pole\n guards to drag it back
      on, AND AS THE LIMB STRAIGHTENS THAT CIRCLE SHRINKS TO A POINT. The swivel\n
      becomes undefined, the fades switch the hint OFF, and the pole is handed to
      a fallback. That handoff is\n the snap users report past ~95% extension. Predict
      the angle and it cannot happen: the angle stays\n defined at any extension,
      and as the circle collapses the resulting POSITION change goes to zero on its\n
      own. Nothing to fade, nothing to hand off, nothing to snap.\n ================================================================================================\n\n
      THIS MODEL READS POSITIONS ONLY, AND THAT IS A SCAR RATHER THAN A SIMPLIFICATION.\n\n
      It briefly carried 27 more features describing the hands ORIENTATION (its rotation
      relative to its own\n T-pose, in the body frame). They measured beautifully
      -- 2.12 % against this model's 3.45 % -- and IN A\n HEADSET THEY PUT THE ELBOWS
      UP BY THE EARS, near-inverted, on almost every frame. Two things were wrong,\n
      and both were knowable in advance:\n\n   1. THE T-POSE THEY DIVIDED BY WAS
      NOT RELIABLY A T-POSE. BasisLocalAvatarDriver calls\n      ResetAvatarAnimator()
      -- literally commented \"Exit T-Pose\" -- BEFORE it builds the rig, so bone\n     
      rotations read at job-build time are not guaranteed to be the rest pose the
      model was fitted\n      against. Divide by the wrong rest and the orientation
      features are not noisy. They are CONFIDENTLY\n      WRONG, which is the only
      kind of wrong that survives a test suite.\n\n   2. THE CORPUS SAID SO, IN WRITING,
      BEFORE ANY OF THIS WAS BUILT. Tests/MocapCorpus~/NOTICE.md:\n      \"A mocap
      hand is not a controller... A VR controller's rotation is a GRIP convention.
      Anything in\n      the IK that reads the hand's rotation is being fed a convention
      it was not designed for, and a\n      result that hinges on it MUST BE CONFIRMED
      IN A HEADSET BEFORE IT IS BELIEVED.\"\n\n Neither can touch a POSITION. A limb's
      geometry is anatomy and it transfers; a bone's rotation is a\n modelling convention
      and it does not. So this model needs no T-pose, reads no rotation, and has
      nothing\n left of that kind to get wrong. If the orientation block is ever
      revived it needs a rest pose taken from\n TposeBoneSnapshot (which is captured
      while the avatar is provably T-posed) and an in-headset A/B before\n one word
      of its accuracy is believed.\n\n ACCURACY -- elbow position error, % of limb
      length, measured in BasisMocapMotionQualityTests:\n     no hint at all ...............
      21.74 %\n     what this replaced ........... the bend LOOKUP + chicken-wing
      flare: 6.62 %, 34 pops\n     THIS MODEL ................... 3.45 %   (4.76
      % leave-one-CLIP-out, so it generalises)\n     a real elbow tracker ........
      1.06 %\n\n SMOOTH BY CONSTRUCTION. A polynomial is C-infinity. There is no
      fade to tune here and no discontinuity\n to fade -- the derivative simply exists,
      everywhere.\n\n The coefficients are fitted to the HARNESS'S OWN DUMPED FEATURES,
      and that is not an accident. The first\n attempt fitted in a separate pipeline
      and scored 3.77 % there and 31 % in the harness -- the two\n disagreed about
      the mirror, and a mismatch in any ONE of handedness / body frame / mirror silently\n
      poisons the model. The harness dumps the exact inputs it feeds this function,
      Python fits on those,\n codegen emits this file. NEVER RE-FIT IN A DIFFERENT
      FRAME FROM THE ONE YOU EVALUATE IN.\n DO NOT HAND-EDIT THE COEFFICIENTS --
      re-fit and re-generate."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.BasisArmSwivelModel
    MemberName: SwivelRad
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 1
    ParamNames:
    - _
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 1
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "The elbows swivel angle, in radians, about the shoulder->hand axis.\n\n
      THE INPUT IS IN THE BODY FRAME AND MIRRORED, so both sides share one model:\n  
      tipLocal   (hand - shoulder), in the body frame, divided by limb length, with
      +x OUTWARD (negate x\n              for the LEFT limb), +y UP, +z FORWARD.\n\n
      The CALLER mirrors the result back: negate the returned angle for the left
      limb."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
