  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.BasisElbowDragCore
    MemberName: BasisElbowDragCore
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 1
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Temporal DRAG on the no-elbow-tracker arm pole: the elbow trails the
      field instead of snapping to it.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      WHY THE ARM HAD NOTHING LIKE THIS, AND WHY THE TWO THINGS THAT LOOK LIKE IT
      ARE NOT.\n\n User, in a headset: \"when they dont have a hint tracker we should
      add some smoothing (drag) to help\n mask us waving our hands and the elbow
      going crazy.\"\n\n   * BasisElbowSwingCapCore is a GAIN cap -- bend rotation
      per unit HAND rotation, deliberately with\n     no dt at all. Its own header
      says it \"SELF-SCALES: a fast hand earns a proportionally fast elbow,\n    
      so there is NO lag on ordinary reaching\". That is exactly the right contract
      for killing a core\n     flip and exactly the wrong one for a wave: wave twice
      as fast and it licenses twice the elbow\n     speed. It bounds the elbow against
      the HAND, and a wave moves the hand.\n\n   * The old SmoothElbowSwivel One-Euro
      (BasisSwivelFilterCore) would be worse than nothing here.\n     Its cutoff
      is `minCutoff + beta * |velocity|` -- it OPENS UP as the signal speeds up,
      on the\n     premise that fast motion is intent worth tracking. BasisElbowFlareCore
      already wrote the\n     epitaph: \"that One-Euro deliberately OPENS its cutoff
      on sustained swivel velocity so a real\n     reach does not lag, and this noise
      arrives precisely when the arm is moving.\" A speed-adaptive\n     filter stops
      filtering exactly when the user is waving, which is the one moment this is
      for.\n\n So neither a gain cap nor a One-Euro can do it, and the arm shipped
      with no temporal filter at all.\n What is missing is plain DRAG: a lag with
      a FIXED time constant, which is what physical inertia is.\n A real forearm
      has mass, so it trails a thrashing hand and settles; slow deliberate motion
      outruns\n the lag almost entirely, because the error a first-order lag holds
      is proportional to SPEED.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      IT IS THE SAME OPERATION AS THE CAP, WITH A MULTIPLY WHERE THE CAP HAS A CLAMP.\n\n
      Both work in the tangent frame perpendicular to the shoulder->hand axis, where
      the single angle\n `ang` is \"how far the bend must swivel THIS FRAME to sit
      on the field\". The cap clamps that angle;\n this scales it. Scaling by alpha
      in (0,1] is a first-order low-pass on the pole, and because the\n frame is
      rebuilt from the CURRENT axis every frame, bulk motion -- a body turn, the
      hand crossing\n the room -- is carried for free and is never what gets lagged.
      Only the swivel is.\n\n Applied to the POLE (the hint handed to the arm solve),
      not to the solved elbow, so the HAND is\n untouched by construction: the two-bone
      solve puts the hand on target whatever the pole says.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      A BODY TURN COSTS NOTHING, BECAUSE THE CARRIED POLE IS ROTATED BY THE BODY
      FIRST.\n\n The transport here is a PROJECTION onto the current axis, which
      does not rotate the carried pole\n about the vertical. On its own that reproduces
      the defect BasisSwivelSmootherCore's header\n describes -- \"a swivel measured
      against a world axis is not invariant under a body turn ... the\n smoother
      then drags the limb toward a stale pole\" -- because the field IS body-framed,
      so a pure\n yaw moves the target while a world-space pole stays put, and the
      whole turn reads as error.\n\n Measured with the world-frame version, hand
      held still relative to a spinning body:\n\n      90 deg/s turn:  0.86 cm of
      elbow lag at 2.5 Hz, 1.81 cm at 1.25 Hz\n     180 deg/s turn:  1.71 cm            
      at 2.5 Hz, 3.57 cm at 1.25 Hz\n\n It scales with the time constant, so doubling
      the damping doubled it, and it would have doubled\n again by ~0.6 Hz. So the
      pole is now carried through `bodyDelta` before the projection, which\n cancels
      a rigid turn EXACTLY rather than damping it: measured 0.00 cm at every rate
      and every drag\n setting. The drag now only ever lags what the arm does RELATIVE
      TO THE BODY, which is the only\n thing it was ever meant to lag."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
