  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.BasisElbowFieldModel
    MemberName: BasisElbowFieldModel
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 1
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: " <summary>\n Where the elbow goes for a user with NO elbow tracker.
      It predicts the elbow's POSITION and\n projects it onto the reachable circle.
      It does not predict an angle, and that is the point.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      WHY THE PREVIOUS MODEL (BasisArmSwivelModel) FLIPPED, AND WHY NO REFIT COULD
      HAVE SAVED IT.\n\n With the shoulder and hand fixed the elbow lies on a CIRCLE,
      so its redundancy is ONE SCALAR --\n the swivel angle. That reframe was right,
      and predicting the angle really does kill the snap at\n full extension. But
      AN ANGLE HAS TO BE MEASURED FROM SOMETHING, and that reference direction is\n
      where the whole thing came apart:\n\n     u = normalize(down - axis * dot(down,
      axis))\n\n |u| is the sine of the arm's angle off vertical. IT IS ZERO WHEN
      THE HAND IS DIRECTLY BELOW THE\n SHOULDER -- and measured across the 55,140-frame
      corpus, |u| < 0.2 on 29.7% OF REAL HUMAN FRAMES,\n with a minimum of 0.001.
      On nearly a third of poses the swivel was being measured against a\n direction
      that barely exists, and `normalizesafe` then hard-snapped it to a fallback.\n\n
      Instrumented, on the shipped model: the hand sways 3 cm fore-aft under the
      shoulder -- standing\n still, arms relaxed, the commonest pose in VR -- and
      the elbow swings 49 degrees, travelling\n 19.6 cm. That is the flip.\n\n AND
      IT IS NOT A CHOICE OF REFERENCE. The pole is a unit tangent vector on the sphere
      of hand\n directions, so by the HAIRY BALL THEOREM every continuous choice
      of it vanishes SOMEWHERE. (The\n same fact in coordinates: sin and cos fitted
      as independent affine functions have a common zero\n LINE -- two planes meet
      in a line.) The singularity cannot be deleted. It can only be MOVED.\n The
      shipped model moved it onto the rest pose.\n\n A POSITION IS NOT A TANGENT
      VECTOR, so it carries no such obstruction. Predict the elbow's\n position,
      project it onto the circle, and the only degeneracy left is \"the predicted
      elbow lands\n ON the shoulder->hand axis\" -- which is 0.036% of the reachable
      workspace.\n\n BUT THE PROJECTED BEND IS A TANGENT VECTOR AGAIN, and Poincare-Hopf
      says any bend field over the\n sphere of hand directions has total index 2:
      TWO zeros per reach shell, somewhere. This model's\n sit at hand ACROSS-THE-BODY-AND-UP
      (-0.88, 0.47, -0.09, deepest at 0.75 reach) and at a shallow\n valley DOWN-AND-BACK
      behind the hip. They cannot be deleted, only placed; everything below is\n
      about what happens NEAR them.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      WHY THE FADE THIS MODEL SHIPPED WITH IS GONE (2026-07-17, \"big arm swings
      flip drastically\").\n\n It used to blend the projected bend toward a fixed
      rest pole below a conditioning of 0.10:\n\n     normalizesafe(primary * w +
      rest * (1 - w), rest)\n\n A LERP OF TWO UNIT DIRECTIONS PASSES THROUGH ZERO
      WHERE THEY ARE ANTIPODAL AND w CROSSES 0.5.\n \"Fade, never gate\" was the
      intent; an antipodal lerp IS a gate -- normalize(~0) flips the whole\n output
      the moment the blend crosses its cancellation. And that cancellation surface
      does not sit\n at the model's zeros, where distrust is deserved -- it cuts
      through HEALTHY workspace where the\n model still has a 3-6 cm lever, exactly
      where dot(primary, rest) = -1 happens to fall:\n\n   * hand swinging ACROSS
      the body at 0.45 reach: elbow teleports 18.4 then 28.7 cm in single\n     0.5-degree
      hand steps (hint direction jumps 73 degrees in one frame), at conditioning
      0.06;\n   * the DOWN-AND-BACK follow-through of a big swing at 0.65 reach:
      same event, 108 degrees.\n\n That is the reported flip, reproduced deterministically
      -- and the fade also LOST its own\n bargain: under 2 mm of hand noise inside
      its band it moved the elbow up to 25.5 deg (p99 6.5)\n against 8.1 deg (p99
      3.2) without it. The rest pole now serves only as the normalizesafe\n fallback
      AT the zeros themselves.\n\n MEASURED, full-sphere meridian sweeps at 0.5 deg
      of hand elevation per step, worst single-step\n bend rotation (flip = tens
      of degrees in one step):\n\n                                reach 0.45   reach
      0.65   reach 0.85   noise p99 (2mm, old band)\n     with the fade ..........     
      78 deg      108 deg      114 deg      6.5 deg\n     projection only ........     
      21 deg      166 deg*      20 deg      3.2 deg\n\n     * only within ~1 degree
      of a zero core, where the fade construction was equally violent;\n       everywhere
      the lever is over 0.02 arm lengths the step now stays under ~21 degrees.\n
      ================================================================================================\n\n
      MEASURED, leave-one-CLIP-out over the corpus, elbow position error as % of
      arm length, and\n elbow travel per unit of HAND travel over the whole reachable
      workspace (a real elbow tracks its\n hand at 0.5-1.5x; above ~5x reads as a
      flip):\n\n                                 err     gain p99   >20x       idle-sway
      step\n     BasisArmSwivelModel ....  4.76 %      68 x     1.67 %        1.13
      cm\n     THIS MODEL ............  4.19 %     5.2 x     0.065 %        0.19
      cm\n\n It is more accurate out of sample with TWELVE coefficients against sixty-four.
      The old cubic's\n coefficients ran to 35 not because a human elbow needs a
      cubic, but because it was spending its\n capacity cancelling the singularity
      of its own reference frame.\n\n FITTED ON THE HARNESS'S OWN DUMPED FEATURES
      (BasisMocapAccuracy -> basis_swivel_train.csv), so\n the fit frame and the
      eval frame cannot disagree. A mismatch in any one of handedness / body\n frame
      / mirror does not degrade this kind of model, it produces CONFIDENT GARBAGE
      -- that has\n happened twice on this project. DO NOT HAND-EDIT THE COEFFICIENTS.
      Re-fit and re-generate.\n </summary>"
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
