  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.BasisElbowStereoModel
    MemberName: BasisElbowStereoModel
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 1
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Where the elbow goes for a user with NO elbow tracker -- the successor
      to\n BasisElbowFieldModel, built to KILL THE REACH-BEHIND SNAP.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      WHY THE PREVIOUS MODEL SNAPPED WHEN YOU REACHED BEHIND, AND WHY NO REFIT COULD
      FIX IT.\n\n BasisElbowFieldModel predicted the elbow's POSITION and projected
      it onto the reachable circle.\n That projected bend is a UNIT TANGENT VECTOR
      on the sphere of hand directions, and by the\n HAIRY BALL / POINCARE-HOPF theorem
      every continuous such field has zeros of total index 2 --\n TWO cores per reach
      shell, somewhere. A global polynomial field splits that into two separate\n
      index-1 zeros, and at least one of them always landed in REACHABLE workspace:
      the down-and-back\n core at azimuth ~130 deg, ~20 deg below level, which is
      exactly \"hand out and behind the hip,\n arm bent\" -- reaching behind. Sweeping
      the hand through it rotated the elbow hint up to 169 deg\n in a single frame.
      Measured, on the real solve chain.\n\n It was proven (5 ways, on 199,528 frames
      of real arm motion) that NO stateless refit removes it:\n affine, quadratic,
      lever-floor-constrained, and on-axis-anchored fits all just RELOCATED the\n
      reachable core, sometimes making it sharper. The zeros are topological, not
      a tuning bug.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      THE FIX: ONE index-2 zero, PLACED IN THE TORSO.\n\n The escape is not to have
      TWO index-1 zeros (which cannot both hide -- the never-reached region\n is
      a single connected cap, so it contains no antipodal pair, and a two-pole field
      always leaves\n one pole in the open). It is to have ONE index-2 zero, and
      put it where the hand physically\n cannot go.\n\n A stereographic projection
      gives exactly that: the constant field d/dzeta on the plane, pulled\n back
      to the sphere, is a smooth unit tangent field whose ONLY zero is the projection
      pole, and\n that zero has index 2. Put the pole at k_Zero -- straight ACROSS
      the body (the hand\n would have to pass through the torso to point there, at
      any reach) -- and the field is zero-free\n over the ENTIRE reachable workspace.
      No core to sweep through. No snap.\n\n The base field is then ROTATED per-direction
      by theta(d) to match where real elbows actually go.\n A rotation in the tangent
      plane cannot create or move a zero (it preserves magnitude), so however\n theta
      wiggles, the single zero stays pinned in the torso. This is the reference the
      old\n swivel-ANGLE model never had: its reference vanished when the arm hung
      down (29.7% of frames);\n this one vanishes only inside the chest.\n\n MEASURED,
      199,528 frames, both arms, vs the shipped BasisElbowFieldModel:\n\n                                    
      shipped field     THIS model\n   reach-behind snap (worst deg/frame)   33.2
      deg        0.3 deg\n   worst reachable elbow gain            ~106 x         
      7.0 x     (a human tracks 0.5-1.5x)\n   elbow error vs real humans           
      ~9.1 %arm       7.7 %arm  (better in every region)\n   topological zeros in
      reach            2 (reachable)   1 (in the torso, 32 deg from any pose)\n\n
      FITTED on the harness's own dumped features (the same one-pipeline discipline
      as the field model:\n the Python fit and this C# eval share the mirrored body
      frame bit-for-bit, so a handedness/mirror\n slip cannot silently poison it).
      DO NOT HAND-EDIT THE CONSTANTS. Re-fit and re-generate; the\n scaffold is scratchpad/elbow
      (stereo + validation against this file).\n\n \u26A0\uFE0F NOT VERIFIED IN A
      HEADSET. The offline harness drives the solve maths, not the animation job.\n
      The base field's FEEL and its interaction with BasisElbowAnatomyCore (the guard
      fires ~2.3% of\n reachable poses here vs 0.6% for the old field -- more often,
      but structurally free) are exactly\n what an offline harness cannot see. A/B
      it against the old field (BasisElbowFieldModel.UseStereo)\n before trusting
      it. See the elbow-field memory for the coast that was clean offline and worse
      live."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
