  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.BasisHeadPitchSwingCore
    MemberName: BasisHeadPitchSwingCore
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 1
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Where the eye actually ends up when the gaze pitches.\n\n A head does
      not pitch about ITSELF. It pitches about the base of the neck, which is BELOW
      it \u2014 so the\n eye, sitting on that lever arm, is carried FORWARD as the
      gaze tips down. Look at your own feet and your\n eyes travel a good 10 cm out
      over them; that is not the spine leaning, it is just the neck.\n\n VR gets
      this for free, because the HMD is a physical object riding the same lever arm.
      The spine bend\n even has to SUPPRESS the resulting hunch when a chest tracker
      is present, precisely because a pure\n look-down swings the HMD forward and
      the bend would otherwise misread it as a torso lean (see\n BasisSpineLookDownHunchTests).
      Desktop had no such thing: the eye was pinned over the hips, so the gaze\n
      tipped, the cervical solve hunched the chest, and the head stayed exactly where
      it was \u2014 leaving the neck\n to absorb a translation the head should have
      made itself.\n\n HORIZONTAL ONLY, deliberately. Eye HEIGHT is left strictly
      alone: UnscaledDeviceCoord.y is what\n CapturePlayerHeight reads, and a pitch-varying
      eye height would feed straight into the avatar-rescale\n loop (the one that
      ends in 12 m players). The forward carry is the part that was missing and the
      part\n that is safe."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
