  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.BasisSpineAnatomy
    MemberName: BasisSpineAnatomy
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 1
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "The range of motion of the human spine, per segment, per axis.\n\n
      ================================================================================================\n
      THE TABLE. Degrees; lateral and axial are PER SIDE.\n\n                                       
      flex   ext   lat   AXIAL      corpus p99 (real humans)\n   Lumbar         L1-S1  
      Unity Spine     60    20    25      12      49.2 / 10.7 / 12.5 / 10.2\n   LowerThoracic 
      T7-T12  Unity Chest     25    15    20      20      18.2 / 11.1 / 10.4 / 14.2\n  
      UpperThoracic  T1-T6   Unity UpprCh    15    30    25      15       5.4 / 18.4
      / 13.1 /  6.6\n   Cervical       C1-C7   Unity Neck      50    60    45     
      75      23.8 / 35.7 / 29.5 / 20.8\n\n EVERY LIMIT IS ABOVE THE p99 OF REAL
      HUMAN MOTION. That is the acceptance test, and it is the right\n way round:
      the limits are max-voluntary-ROM from the clinical literature (what a spine
      CAN do), and\n the corpus only gets a veto (what people DID do). Fit the limits
      TO the corpus and you encode\n \"CMU subjects rarely side-bend\" as \"humans
      cannot side-bend\", and then you clip the first VR user who\n leans over to
      look at something. Measured across 35,081 frames the whole table fires on under
      0.6% of\n frames, and only on poses the literature says are impossible.\n\n
      SOURCES, and why the axial column is the one that matters:\n\n   LUMBAR AXIAL
      = 12 deg/side. Fujii 2007, Eur Spine J 16(11):1867 -- in-vivo 3D MRI, at 45
      deg of\n   trunk rotation L1-S1 sums to 7.7 deg/side (1.2-1.7 deg per segment),
      age-invariant. The lumbar\n   facets are near-sagittal: they interlock and
      simply will not permit twist. 12 leaves headroom over\n   both Fujii and the
      corpus's 10.2 p99. THE SHIPPED RIG ALLOWED 25, AND THEN, PAST THE CCD, ANY
      VALUE\n   AT ALL -- the corpus contains a 34.9 deg lumbar twist and the rig
      would reproduce it.\n\n   THORACIC AXIAL = 15-20 deg/side. Thoracic in-vivo
      total ~30 deg/side (Ichikawa 2024), split across\n   the two halves, with the
      upper thorax stiffer because its ribs anchor to the sternum. The ribcage\n  
      roughly HALVES thoracic rotation (Liebsch 2017) -- so do not use rib-stripped
      in-vitro per-segment\n   numbers, which is where the folklore \"the spine twists
      freely\" comes from.\n\n   THORACIC FLEXION = 15-25 deg. The thorax barely
      flexes; the ribs are in the way. The corpus is\n   blunt about it -- during
      a real forward bend the upper thorax contributes NEGATIVE 9.3% of the\n   total,
      i.e. it EXTENDS slightly, to keep the head up. It pitched 5.4 deg at p99 and
      the shipped rig\n   allowed it 60. That gap is the user's \"looking down forces
      the chest to rotate\".\n\n   CERVICAL = the freest segment in the body, and
      the numbers are unremarkable: 50/60/45/75.\n\n   Standing max trunk twist is
      ~70% PELVIS, ~30% SPINE (Taniguchi 2017, PLoS ONE: thorax ~92 deg/side,\n  
      pelvis ~65, spine-relative-to-pelvis only ~26.5). A rig with an unbounded chest-vs-pelvis
      twist is\n   wrong by 2-3x, which is what this table exists to stop.\n ================================================================================================\n\n
      Related: BasisSpineAnatomyCore (the guard), BasisElbowAnatomyCore + BasisLegSolveCore
      (the same\n doctrine on the limbs -- guard the OUTCOME, run LAST, be the identity
      where the pose is legal)."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
