  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.BasisSpineAnatomyCore
    MemberName: BasisSpineAnatomyCore
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 1
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "THE SPINE IS NOT ONE JOINT, AND IT IS NOT A BALL JOINT.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      WHAT SHIPPED, AND WHY IT COULD NOT BE RIGHT:\n\n   1. There was NO AXIAL ROTATION
      LIMIT ANYWHERE IN THE SPINE. In BasisSpineBendCore.ClampAsymmetric,\n     
      the twist component `e.y` was clamped by `maxLat` -- THE LATERAL BEND LIMIT.
      Those are different\n      degrees of freedom of different joints. There was
      no `SpineMaxAxialDeg` to clamp it with; the\n      field did not exist.\n\n  
      2. ONE ROM WAS SHARED BY EVERY SEGMENT. `SpineMaxForwardDeg=60 / Backward=25
      / Lateral=25` were\n      applied identically to the lumbar spine and to the
      upper chest. The lumbar does the great\n      majority of the trunk's flexion
      and almost none of its twist; the upper thorax is the reverse.\n      A single
      triple is wrong for both, and wrong in opposite directions.\n\n   3. AND NONE
      OF IT SURVIVED THE SOLVE ANYWAY. `ClampAsymmetric` limits the PRE-BEND delta,
      and then\n      SolveSequentialSpineIK -- the CCD that actually places the
      head -- runs AFTERWARDS and rotates\n      the same bones with no per-joint
      limit at all. Its only constraints were a 45 deg symmetric cone\n      on the
      neck and a 90 deg cone on the chest. THE LUMBAR SPINE AND THE UPPER CHEST HAD
      NOTHING.\n      The single most rotation-restricted segment in the human trunk
      was the one bone in the chain\n      with no constraint of any kind.\n\n THE
      NUMBERS, MEASURED ON 35,081 FRAMES OF REAL HUMANS (CMU corpus, 64 clips, per-segment
      swing-twist\n against each bone's own rest axis):\n\n                         
      flexion   extension   lateral   AXIAL       what shipped allowed\n     lumbar   
      (Spine)      49.2       10.7       12.5     10.2        60 / 25 / 25 / 25*\n    
      lo-thorac (Chest)      18.2       11.1       10.4     14.2        60 / 25 /
      25 / 25*\n     up-thorac (UpprCh)      5.4       18.4       13.1      6.6       
      60 / 25 / 25 / 25*\n     cervical  (Neck)       23.8       35.7       29.5    
      20.8        45 deg cone, no twist limit\n     (p99 of |angle|, degrees)                           
      *and unbounded once the CCD ran\n\n   The upper chest pitches 5.4 deg at p99
      and was allowed 60. That is \"looking down forces the chest\n   to rotate\".
      The lumbar twists 10.2 deg at p99 and was allowed 25 and then anything at all.\n
      ================================================================================================\n\n
      THE LIMITS COME FROM THE LITERATURE, NOT FROM THE CORPUS, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
      Motion capture\n records what people DID, not what they CAN do -- CMU subjects
      walk around a lab, they do not\n side-bend to end range. Fitting the envelope
      to the corpus would encode \"didn't\" as \"can't\" and\n then clip the first
      VR user who leans sideways. So the limits are max-voluntary-ROM from the\n
      clinical literature (see BasisSpineAnatomy), and the corpus gets a VETO ONLY:
      it is used to prove\n the envelope never fires on real motion. It does not:
      across all 35,081 frames the guard perturbs\n under 0.6% of them on every segment,
      and every frame it does touch is one the literature says is\n impossible --
      a 35 deg lumbar twist (Fujii's in-vivo MRI says 7.7), a 100 deg lumbar flexion.
      Those\n are artifacts of CMU's 3-joint spine, and the shipped rig would have
      reproduced them faithfully.\n\n THE SWING ENVELOPE IS AN ELLIPTICAL CONE, NOT
      A BOX. Clamping flexion, lateral bend and twist\n independently -- which is
      what clamping euler components does -- admits max-flexion AND max-side-bend\n
      AND max-twist simultaneously. No spine does that; you cannot bend fully forward
      and fully sideways at\n once. The cone has four quadrant radii (flexion and
      extension differ; left and right do not), which is\n the standard reach-cone
      construction.\n\n THE TWIST LIMIT IS FLAT, AND THAT TOO IS MEASURED. The hip's
      rotational slack collapses at end-range\n swing (van Arkel 2015) and it was
      tempting to port that here. The corpus says the spine does not do\n that: binned
      by swing magnitude, the lumbar's p99 axial rotation runs 8.7 / 8.7 / 10.6 /
      10.7 / 10.4\n deg from 0-10 deg of swing out to 45-90. It does not collapse.
      So there is no swing-twist coupling in\n this guard, because there is no evidence
      for one.\n\n IT GUARDS THE OUTCOME AND RUNS INSIDE THE CCD, WHICH IS THE ONLY
      PLACE IT CAN WORK. A limit applied\n before the CCD is a suggestion. Applied
      after the CCD it would drag the head off the HMD. Applied\n per-joint INSIDE
      the loop, the residual simply redistributes onto the other vertebrae on the
      next\n sweep -- which is exactly what a real spine does when you ask one segment
      for more than it has.\n\n IT IS THE IDENTITY INSIDE THE ENVELOPE, BIT FOR BIT.
      A legal pose is returned untouched -- not\n re-normalised, not round-tripped
      through a decomposition. A guard that perturbs legal poses to fix\n illegal
      ones is the wrong trade. Outside, it eases in with the same rational saturation
      and slope-1\n handover that BasisElbowAnatomyCore and BasisLegSolveCore.ClampKneeSwivelDeg
      use, so the spine\n arrives at its limit instead of hitting a wall. Slope-1
      means no derivative step, which is the\n difference between a guard and a pop.\n\n
      THE HEAD IS NOT GUARDED, ON PURPOSE. It is welded to the HMD -- commanded,
      not solved. A guard there\n would fight the tracker and drag the view off the
      user's eyes. (And the corpus agrees it would be\n wrong to try: CMU's `Head`
      joint carries real cervical motion, and a C0-C1 envelope clips 29% of it.)\n
      This guards what the solver INVENTS. The same doctrine as the arm: guard the
      elbow, never the hand."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
