  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.BasisSwivelHintCore
    MemberName: BasisSwivelHintCore
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 1
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Builds the exact inputs BasisElbowFieldModel (arm) and BasisLegSwivelModel
      (leg) were FITTED on, and\n turns their prediction into the hint position the
      two-bone solvers want.\n\n The two limbs no longer answer the same KIND of
      question, and that is deliberate. The leg still\n predicts a swivel ANGLE;
      the arm predicts the elbow's POSITION and projects it onto the reachable\n
      circle. An angle must be measured from a reference direction, and every choice
      of reference vanishes\n somewhere on the sphere of tip directions -- the arm's
      old one vanished when the arm hung straight\n down, which is 29.7% of real
      human frames and the commonest pose in VR. The leg's reference is body\n OUTWARD,
      and a leg does not point sideways out of the hip, so its zero sits outside
      the reachable set.\n The arm had nowhere safe to put it. See BasisElbowFieldModel.\n\n
      ================================================================================================\n
      THIS FILE EXISTS SO THE RUNTIME AND THE FIT CANNOT DISAGREE.\n\n The models
      are polynomials in three numbers. Get any ONE of handedness, body frame or
      mirror wrong and\n the model does not degrade -- it produces CONFIDENT GARBAGE,
      which is the only kind of wrong that gets\n past a green test suite. That has
      now happened twice on this project:\n\n   * a fit done in a separate pipeline
      scored 3.77 % there and 31 % in the harness, because the two\n     disagreed
      about the mirror. The predicted swivel came out 145 degrees off. Nothing crashed.\n\n  
      * an earlier version of THIS FILE fed the models 27 extra features describing
      the tip's ORIENTATION,\n     divided by a T-pose read at job-build time. BasisLocalAvatarDriver
      exits T-pose BEFORE it builds the\n     rig, so that \"rest pose\" was not
      reliably a rest pose -- and in a headset the elbows sat up by the\n     ears,
      near-inverted, on almost every frame, while every test in the suite stayed
      green. The corpus\n     had warned about exactly this in writing (NOTICE.md:
      \"a mocap hand is not a controller\").\n\n So the feature construction lives
      in ONE place, it reads POSITIONS ONLY, and\n BasisSwivelHintConformanceTests
      pins it against the harness's own construction (BasisMocapAccuracy,\n which
      IS the fit pipeline).\n ================================================================================================"
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.BasisSwivelHintCore
    MemberName: ElbowTuckWeight
    Kind: Field
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 1
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "THE ARM NO LONGER HAS ONE, AND THAT IS THE FIX, NOT AN OMISSION.\n\n
      This used to be a HARD GATE at 0.20 in BasisFullBodyIK.SolveHand: below it
      the hint was dropped\n ENTIRELY and the elbow fell back to whatever the animation
      clip happened to be doing. Two\n unrelated poles, switched between on a boolean
      -- which is the definition of a pop, and which\n LegHint's own comment below
      already says in as many words.\n\n It is gone because BasisElbowFieldModel
      has nothing to be unconfident ABOUT: it predicts a\n POSITION, so its only
      degeneracy is geometric (the predicted elbow landing on the arm's own\n axis),
      it is measurable, and it is measure-zero -- the model falls back to its rest
      pole ONLY\n on those exact cores. (It used to FADE toward that pole below a
      0.10 lever instead, and the\n fade's antipodal lerp was itself a hidden gate:
      it teleported the elbow 28 cm/frame on big\n cross-body swings. See the field
      model's header.)"
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.BasisSwivelHintCore
    MemberName: ElbowDownWeight
    Kind: Field
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 1
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "ELBOW-DOWN ON A STRAIGHT ARM. The field model predicts the CORPUS-MEAN
      elbow, and CMU contains\n essentially no straight-arm lateral holds -- a T-pose
      is a calibration pose, not a motion -- so the\n mean there is dragged backward
      by the reaching motions that DO trail the elbow. Measured on the\n shipping
      model: at a lateral straight arm it wants the elbow 52 deg BACK of straight
      down, where a\n real one hangs down.\n\n That error is nearly free in ELBOW
      POSITION and very expensive in BONE ROLL. At full extension the\n elbow's lever
      arm is ~2 cm, so 52 deg of azimuth buys almost no displacement -- it is paid
      as humeral\n ROLL, which moves no joint (so every position gate in this repo
      is structurally blind to it) and reads\n as a twisted, pinched deltoid. Measured
      52 deg of permanent humeral twist on an elbow-down bind.\n\n Gated on EXTENSION,
      not on confidence: the model has nothing to be unconfident about (see above),
      but\n its positional authority genuinely collapses as the arm straightens,
      and that is exactly where the\n anatomy it failed to learn -- gravity and the
      carrying angle hang the elbow down -- takes over. Below\n k_ElbowDownReachStart
      this is the exact identity, so the fitted field is untouched everywhere it
      was\n actually trained.\n\n Same shape as the tuck: an UN-NORMALISED perpendicular
      projection, so it self-fades to zero as the\n arm approaches the pole's own
      direction and cannot introduce a new zero."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
