  - TypeFullName: Basis.Scripts.Drivers.
    MemberName: SafeFootTargetRotation
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 2
    ParamNames:
    - footRot
    - 0.5f)
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 1
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "The foot target rotation to hand SolveLegs, with the per-avatar calibration
      offset pre-cancelled --\n or the preserve-tip sentinel if the result is not
      a usable rotation.\n\n WHY THIS EXISTS: a NaN here does not degrade the rig,
      it KILLS it. SolveLegs decides \"no rotation\n supplied\" with `sqrMagnitude(tRot)
      < 0.5f` -- and NaN < 0.5f is FALSE, so a NaN target does not trip\n that guard.
      It flows into SolveTwoBone, NaNs the leg bone rotations, and from there the
      rig never\n recovers: zeroing EnableLeftLeg only stops us WRITING, it cannot
      un-poison what is already written.\n That is exactly \"the legs stop falling
      back to the animator when I move, and never come back\".\n\n Two ways a NaN
      gets in, and both are guarded:\n   - the OFFSET is degenerate. A serialized
      Quaternion defaults to (0,0,0,0), not identity, and\n     Quaternion.Inverse
      divides by the squared norm -- inverting it yields NaN. There is a real window\n    
      for this: BasisAnimationRiggingHelper only assigns the offset when the avatar
      HAS that foot mapped,\n     and recalibration/avatar-swap rewrite it live.\n  
      - the foot driver's own rotation is degenerate (a LookRotation on a collapsed
      frame).\n\n NOTE THE COMPARISON SHAPE: `!(x > k)`, never `x < k`. NaN compares
      false to EVERYTHING, so a `<` test\n ACCEPTS NaN. That is precisely the bug
      in SolveLegs' preserveTip check, and the first version of this\n guard repeated
      it. Negating a `>` rejects NaN, zero and denormals in one test.\n\n Falling
      back to the SENTINEL rather than identity matters: identity would hand the
      solve a confidently\n WRONG foot rotation, while the sentinel restores exactly
      the old, known-good behaviour (the animation's\n foot rotation). Foot rotation
      degrades; walking never breaks."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
