  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.Mocap.BasisBodySegment
    MemberName: BasisBodySegment
    Kind: Enum
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "IS THIS POSE A BODY A HUMAN COULD BE IN? -- asked of the WHOLE skeleton
      at once, for the first time.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      WHY THIS EXISTS. Every anatomical guard in this project was written AFTER a
      user complained from\n inside a headset:\n\n   * the KNEE got its anterior
      half-space guard (BasisLegSolveCore) early;\n   * the ELBOW got BasisElbowAnatomyCore
      only after \"the arms are able to get in rotations that are\n     not possible...
      the elbows point to the sky\";\n   * the SPINE got BasisSpineAnatomyCore after
      \"looking down forces the chest to rotate\".\n\n Each of those is one joint,
      found one complaint at a time. NOBODY HAS EVER LOOKED AT THE WHOLE\n SKELETON
      AT ONCE, and the by-contrast list is long: BasisLegDiagnostics says so in its
      own comments\n (\"Nothing constrains the femur against the pelvis anywhere
      in the solve, so these are logged rather\n than guarded\"), and the 2026-07-19
      audit found wrist, tracked ankle, toes, hips-vs-root, head-vs-neck,\n upper
      arm and upper leg with no ROM clamp of any kind.\n\n AND NOTHING AT ALL LOOKS
      AT THE BODY AS A SOLID. A per-joint envelope cannot see an arm passing\n through
      the chest, a forearm through a thigh in a deep crouch, or the feet swapping
      sides -- each\n individual joint is inside its own limits the whole time. That
      is a WHOLE-BODY question and it needs\n a whole-body measurement.\n\n So there
      are two independent checks here, and they fail for genuinely different reasons:\n\n  
      (A) SELF-INTERSECTION -- the body as capsules, does any limb pass through another
      or through the\n       torso. Geometry only; no anatomy, no thresholds from
      the literature.\n   (B) ROM CONFORMANCE -- every joint's angle in its own parent
      frame, against an anatomical envelope.\n ================================================================================================\n\n
      WHERE THE LIMITS COME FROM, AND THE ONE PLACE THIS DISAGREES WITH ITS OWN BRIEF.\n\n
      The instinct is \"derive the limits from the corpus\". THIS PROJECT HAS ALREADY
      LITIGATED THAT AND\n COME DOWN THE OTHER WAY, and the reasoning is in BasisSpineAnatomy's
      header:\n\n     \"Motion capture records what people DID, not what they CAN
      do -- CMU subjects walk around a lab,\n      they do not side-bend to end range.
      Fitting the envelope to the corpus would encode 'didn't' as\n      'can't'
      and then clip the first VR user who leans sideways.\"\n\n So the limits are
      max-voluntary ROM from the clinical literature, and THE CORPUS HOLDS A VETO:
      it\n measures what real humans actually did, and if a limit fires in the FAT
      of that distribution the\n limit is wrong, not the human. That is the same
      requirement as \"a limit that clamps poses real humans\n actually made is a
      wrong limit\" -- it just runs the test in the honest direction. The measured\n
      distribution (p50 / p95 / p99 / min / max, per channel, per corpus tier) is
      what\n BasisRomAccumulator exists to produce, and it is reported whether or
      not anything gates.\n\n AND THREE OF THE CHANNELS HAVE A KNOWN ANSWER ALREADY,
      WHICH IS WHAT PROVES THE RULER.\n This project has shipped lying metrics before
      -- a pop detector whose count was identical whether its\n cause was present
      or absent, a smoothness metric that scored over-smoothed mush above a real
      human --\n so a new measurement is worth nothing until it reproduces a number
      somebody already established:\n\n   * ELBOW CEILING. BasisElbowAnatomyCore
      measured 55,140 frames of real arm motion and found the\n     worst violation
      of \"the elbow never rises above the higher of shoulder and hand\" is +0.015
      arm\n     lengths. BasisRomChannel.LeftElbowCeilingFracArm measures exactly
      that quantity.\n\n     \u26A0 IT DID NOT REPRODUCE, THE RULER WAS RIGHT, AND
      THE SHIPPED CODE HAS SINCE BEEN FIXED.\n     Ran 2026-07-22. In the frame the
      guard enforced AT THE TIME (PlayerUp, i.e. world up for an\n     upright root)
      the root tier's worst was 0.0525, not 0.015 -- past the guard's own 0.05 soft\n    
      margin. In the TORSO's frame the same tier gives exactly 0.0150. The published
      number had\n     therefore been measured in a torso-relative frame while the
      guard enforced the law in world\n     space, and on a bent-over torso the two
      disagree by the whole trunk lean (posture/ tier: +0.3723\n     world, -0.3713
      torso, on the SAME frames -- the sign flips).\n     BasisArmSolveCore now passes
      BasisElbowAnatomyCore a TORSO up (chest->neck), so the guard and the\n    
      published evidence finally describe the same law.\n     BasisRomChannel.LeftElbowCeilingTorsoFracArm
      is the channel that tracks shipped\n     behaviour; the world channel above
      is kept as the historical comparison baseline. Note that\n     NEITHER NUMBER
      MOVED when the guard was fixed, and that is not a bug: both are measurements
      of\n     what the CORPUS did, and the humans did not change frame. The full
      evidence is in\n     BasisBodyPlausibilityTests's elbow test.\n   * SPINE.
      The lumbar / lower-thoracic / cervical channels do not re-implement anything
      -- they call\n     BasisSpineAnatomy.BuildRestFrame and BasisSpineAnatomyCore.Decompose,
      THE SHIPPING FUNCTIONS, so\n     they must reproduce the p99 table in BasisSpineAnatomy's
      header (lumbar 49.2 flex / 10.7 ext /\n     12.5 lat / 10.2 axial, and so on
      for the rest).\n   * KNEE ANTERIOR. BasisBvhLoader.Validate already asserts
      a majority of sampled frames put the knee\n     in front of the hip->ankle
      axis. BasisRomChannel.LeftKneeAnteriorDot is the same\n     half-space, measured
      on every frame rather than a sample.\n\n EVERY ANGLE IS TAKEN FROM POSITIONS,
      NOT FROM BONE-LOCAL AXES. A bone's local frame is a rig\n convention and does
      not transfer -- this project has been bitten by that repeatedly, which is why\n
      BasisSpineAnatomy.BuildRestFrame builds its axes from positions and why the
      arm swivel model is\n position-only. The pelvis and chest frames here are built
      the same way (up from the segment's own long\n axis, right from the hip sockets
      / shoulder joints), so the numbers carry no bind-pose dependency and\n mean
      the same thing on a CMU skeleton and on a shipped avatar. The ONE exception
      is\n BasisRomChannel.LeftFemurTwistDeg, which is a rotation and therefore convention-bound
      --\n it is reported and never gated, exactly as BasisLegDiagnostics does with
      the same quantity.\n\n AND IT MEASURES A POSE, NOT A CLIP. BasisBodyFrame is
      a plain array in BasisMocapJoint\n order, so the SOLVER'S OUTPUT goes through
      the identical maths as a real human -- which is the whole\n point. The corpus
      run is the ruler being calibrated; the solved run is the thing being measured."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
