  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.Mocap.BasisSegmentGroup
    MemberName: BasisSegmentGroup
    Kind: Enum
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "THE MOCAP SUBJECT IS WEARING THEIR OWN BODY. A VR USER NEVER IS.\n\n
      Every accuracy number this project quotes was measured with a CMU subject driving
      THEIR OWN\n proportions: the hand target and the arm it hangs off came from
      the same skeleton, so\n |shoulder->hand| / armLength <= 1 on 100% of frames.
      That is not what the runtime is handed. The\n live rig gets the RAW CONTROLLER
      TARGET -- where the USER's hand is -- against whatever arm the\n AVATAR happens
      to have. Anyone whose real arms are longer than their avatar's is beyond that
      ratio\n permanently, and that gap has already shipped a bug: the swivel polynomials
      had no domain clamp, so\n out there a 3rd-order fit with coefficients up to
      15 was a random number generator, and the elbows\n went up by the ears in a
      headset while every test in the suite stayed green (see\n BasisLegSwivelModel's
      THE DOMAIN CLAMP block and BasisSwivelHintConformanceTests' header).\n\n This
      file manufactures the missing input. It takes a clip and produces a PROPORTION-RESCALED\n
      VARIANT: the same motion performed by a different body. Pair the rescaled clip's
      SKELETON with the\n original clip's HAND AND FOOT TARGETS and the harness is
      finally driving the solver the way the\n runtime drives it -- one body's limbs
      chasing another body's effectors.\n\n WHAT \"RESCALE\" MEANS HERE, EXACTLY\n  
      Every bone keeps its WORLD DIRECTION and changes its LENGTH. Joint positions
      are rebuilt outward\n   from the hips along the source clip's own bone vectors,
      each multiplied by its group's scale.\n   Because no direction moves, every
      joint's world ROTATION is unchanged and is copied through\n   verbatim -- the
      pose is preserved, only the skeleton's dimensions differ. That is the property
      the\n   whole measurement rests on: if the rescale perturbed the motion, every
      mismatch number taken from\n   it would be measuring the perturbation instead
      of the mismatch.\n\n BIT-EXACT AT 1.0x, AND NOT BY SHORT-CIRCUITING\n   The
      rebuild accumulates a DISPLACEMENT (`delta`), not an absolute position:\n      
      delta[child] = delta[parent] + bone * (scale - 1)\n       newPos[j]    = srcPos[j]
      + delta[j]\n   At scale 1.0 the factor is exactly 0f, so every delta stays
      exactly zero and every position comes\n   back exactly equal to the source
      -- while the full code path still RUNS. The obvious alternative,\n   `newPos[child]
      = newPos[parent] + bone * scale`, is NOT exact at 1.0 (a + (b - a) != b in\n  
      floating point), and an `if (IsIdentity) return copy;` early-out would make
      the identity test\n   guard nothing at all. This project has already shipped
      two metrics that were quietly lying -- a\n   pop detector whose count was identical
      whether its cause was present or absent, and a smoothness\n   score that preferred
      over-smoothed mush to a real human -- so the ruler gets guarded, and the\n  
      guard has to be able to fail.\n\n THE PELVIS DOES NOT MOVE, AND THE CLIP IS
      NOT RE-GROUNDED. Shortening the legs leaves the hips where\n the human's hips
      were, so the feet float. That is deliberate and it is stated rather than silently\n
      fixed: nothing this file's consumers measure is floor-relative (BasisBvhLoader.Validate
      is entirely\n relative/rotational, BasisMocapAccuracy takes every root from
      the clip and every error as a\n distance), and a grounding pass would be an
      unmeasured extra transform sitting between the corpus\n and the numbers. A
      floor-contact consumer must ground it itself.\n\n EDITOR-ONLY, IN THE RUNTIME
      ASSEMBLY, for the reason written into BasisBvhLoader's header: a mocap\n analysis
      layer has no business in a player build, but Basis.Framework.IK.Tests does
      not reference\n BasisEditor, so putting the maths there would force the tests
      to hand-mirror it -- the drift trap\n the whole core/sweep/gate convention
      exists to avoid."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
