  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.Mocap.
    MemberName: static
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 2
    ParamNames:
    - pass
    - frames"
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 1
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "The error ceiling for a synthetic-tracker row, as a FRACTION OF THE
      LIMB, derived from the\n device's own parameters rather than picked. Retune
      a preset and the ceiling follows it, so the\n bound can never silently disagree
      with the model it is bounding -- the same reasoning\n BasisSyntheticTracker.Slip01MaxRatePerSecond
      exists for.\n\n THE DERIVATION. The joint lies on a circle of radius r about
      the root->tip axis, and r <= half\n the limb length (the limit as the limb
      folds; a right-angled limb is nearer 0.35). The only\n device error the POLE
      can express is the angular station about that axis: the mount roll, drawn\n
      once within +-MountRollDeg, plus the session slip, peaking at SlipAroundDeg.
      A station error of\n theta moves the joint 2*r*sin(theta/2) <= r*theta. So
      the angular term, scale-free, is\n 0.5 * (MountRollDeg + SlipAroundDeg) in
      radians.\n\n Plus a flat slack for the terms that are position-space rather
      than angular: jitter (2 mm sigma\n at worst), glitches (3 cm on 10% of samples
      at worst), delivery latency and held samples -- and,\n for the LEG, the stand-off,
      which the arm's perpendicular projection absorbs and the leg's does\n not.
      8% of an 0.85 m leg is 6.8 cm, which covers the 6 cm worst-case stand-off outright.\n\n
      \u26A0 THIS IS A SANITY CEILING, NOT A WIRING CHECK, and the distinction is
      the whole reason it is\n separate from TruthJoint's. TruthJoint's 3 cm / 5
      cm bounds PROVE THE HARNESS IS WIRED: handed\n the real joint the solver must
      reproduce it, and loosening them would destroy the only check that\n says the
      corpus numbers mean anything. A 4 cm stand-off alone would blow them, so a
      tracker row\n must never be measured against them -- it would fail for an entirely
      correct reason and get\n \"fixed\" by widening the one bound in this file that
      must not move.\n\n These bounds instead say only \"the device model has not
      gone berserk\". Every term above is a\n worst case applied to a MEAN, so real
      numbers should sit far below; if one trips, check it\n against the model before
      touching the constant -- but widening THIS constant costs nothing that\n widening
      TruthJoint's would. The real correctness check for these rows is a comparison
      Gate\n structurally cannot make from a single summary: a tracker row must land
      BETWEEN TruthJoint and\n None, and worse as the mount gets worse. That belongs
      in the test that sweeps the rows."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.Mocap.BasisPostureSample
    MemberName: BasisPostureSample
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "The pelvis's posture problem, reduced to four numbers -- and the SAME
      four the live rig can actually\n compute, which is the only reason any of this
      transfers.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      THE PROBLEM. A VR user gives you a head and nothing else. Two completely different
      bodies produce the\n same low head:\n\n     WAIST-BEND   spine folds forward,
      pelvis stays HIGH and slides BACK over the heels.\n     SQUAT / SIT  spine
      stays stacked, pelvis rides the head almost straight DOWN.\n\n Measured on
      the CMU corpus, the pelvis-drop-per-head-drop coupling is 0.23 for the first
      and 0.88 for\n the second. The shipped rig applies ONE saturating law to both,
      so it cannot help being wrong twice --\n and it is: on a deep squat it holds
      the pelvis 32.8 cm above where a real one goes, and the head is\n welded to
      the HMD, so the spine and neck have to find every one of those centimetres
      by ROTATING.\n That is the \"tortoise\", and it is arithmetic.\n\n THE DISCRIMINATOR
      IS HOW FAR FORWARD THE HEAD WENT. A squat barely travels; a waist-bend travels
      a\n long way. The rig's vertical law never looks at that, which is precisely
      why it cannot tell them apart.\n ================================================================================================\n\n
      EVERY QUANTITY HERE IS AVAILABLE AT RUNTIME, and everything is normalised by
      the user's own standing\n head height, so the model is SCALE-FREE -- a child
      avatar and a giant get the same posture, not the\n same centimetres. (The swivel
      models normalise by limb length for the identical reason.)\n\n   Origin   the
      SUPPORT BASE: the midpoint of the feet. The rig already has this\n           
      (BasisLocalVirtualSpineDriver uses feetMidXZ when the feet are tracked, and
      HeadBaselineXZ\n            -- a slow horizontal baseline of the standing spot
      -- when they are not).\n   Drop     (standing head height - current head height)
      / standing head height.\n   Fwd      how far the head has travelled horizontally
      FROM that support base, over the same height.\n            Unsigned: the DIRECTION
      is carried separately, so no body-facing frame is needed anywhere.\n           
      This matters -- a facing built from the pelvis would be circular, since the
      pelvis is the\n            thing being solved.\n\n The two OUTPUTS are in the
      same units, and the horizontal one is SIGNED ALONG THE HEAD'S OWN LEAN\n DIRECTION,
      so a negative value means the pelvis is BEHIND the support base -- counterbalancing,
      which\n is what a bending human's pelvis actually does."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.Mocap.BasisPostureSample
    MemberName: HeadPitch
    Kind: Field
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "CANDIDATE THIRD FEATURE: the head's pitch below horizontal, in radians.
      Positive = looking down.\n\n The hope is that it separates the two postures
      where (drop, lean) cannot. Squat down to pick\n something up and your torso
      stays upright, so your head only has to TILT to see the floor. Bend at\n the
      waist to do the same job and your whole head is CARRIED down by a pitching
      torso. Same head\n height, same lean, different head angle -- in principle.\n\n
      In principle. Gaze is voluntary, so this may be mostly noise. That is what
      the fit is for; it is\n dumped and TESTED before a single line of runtime plumbing
      is written for it."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
