  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.Motion.BasisMocapMotionQuality
    MemberName: BasisMocapMotionQuality
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 1
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\"Is it human? Is it natural?\" -- answered by comparing the solver's
      joint motion against A REAL\n HUMAN DOING THE SAME MOTION, frame for frame.\n\n
      This is what makes the question answerable instead of a matter of taste. Every
      metric here is a\n DIFFERENTIAL against ground truth, so there are no invented
      thresholds to argue about: the\n question is not \"is 1200 units of jerk a
      lot\" but \"did OUR elbow move like THAT elbow did,\n given the same hand to
      follow\".\n\n The joints scored are the ones the SOLVER INVENTS -- the elbow
      and the knee. The hand and the\n foot are commanded by trackers; if those are
      wrong it is a wiring bug, and the accuracy gate\n already catches it. Nothing
      tells the elbow where to go, so the elbow is where naturalness\n lives or dies.\n\n
      WHY THE JERK RATIO IS THE FLAGSHIP, AND WHY IT IS TWO-SIDED:\n Over-smooth
      a real human elbow and it scores BETTER than the human on every smoothness
      metric\n there is (measured: jerk 1171 -> 95, SPARC -3.41 -> -2.62). A one-sided
      gate would therefore\n reward the mushy, laggy, robotic motion we are trying
      to eliminate, and fail the actual human.\n So the gate is a BAND: the solver's
      elbow must be roughly as alive as the human's -- not calmer,\n not busier.
      See BasisMotionQuality for the full argument."
    Remarks: 
