  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.Motion.BasisMotionQuality
    MemberName: BasisMotionQuality
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 1
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: " <summary>\n Scores how a joint MOVED, as opposed to where it ended
      up.\n\n Every other IK gate in this project asks \"is this pose right\". None
      of them ask \"is this motion\n right\", and the two are genuinely independent:
      a solved elbow can be 2 cm accurate on every\n single frame and still buzz
      at 30 Hz, sit on a plateau, or arrive 150 ms late. Position accuracy\n cannot
      see any of that, because each frame, taken alone, looks fine. The badness only
      exists\n BETWEEN frames.\n\n ============================================================================================\n
      THE ONE THING TO UNDERSTAND BEFORE ADDING A METRIC HERE:\n\n     SMOOTHNESS
      METRICS ARE ONE-SIDED. THEY PUNISH ROUGH AND *REWARD* MUSH.\n\n Measured against
      the CMU corpus, degrading a real human elbow by over-smoothing it with a\n
      150 ms filter makes it score BETTER than the human on every smoothness metric
      there is:\n\n         metric        real human      +150ms over-smooth    
      +plateau     +linearised\n         jerk/L          1171                 95                
      244            237\n         SPARC          -3.41               -2.62             
      -2.34          -2.89\n\n A gate of the form `smoothness <= threshold` therefore
      hands a passing grade to exactly the\n laggy, robotic, dead-feeling motion
      we are trying to eliminate, and FAILS the real human. It is\n worse than no
      gate, because it actively pushes the solver the wrong way.\n\n So: JerkPerLimb
      is gated as a FLOOR as well as a ceiling. A living arm has a characteristic\n
      amount of jerk in it. Too little means something upstream ate the motion.\n\n
      And smoothness alone is never enough -- it must be paired with ShapeDistance
      (which catches\n energy in the wrong bands) and with a lag measurement from
      BasisMotionResponse, or the cheapest\n way to pass every gate is to smooth
      harder, forever.\n ============================================================================================\n
      </summary>"
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
