  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.Motion.float[]+BasisMotionResponse
    MemberName: BasisMotionResponse
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 1
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "The \"is it blending at the correct speed\" engine.\n\n A blend's speed
      is only meaningful if it is the SAME speed on every machine. Basis ships to\n
      headsets running 72, 80, 90, 120 and 144 Hz, and to desktop at whatever the
      monitor does. A\n smoother written as\n\n     t = saturate(dt * speed);  x
      = lerp(x, target, t);          // WRONG\n\n has a time constant that is a function
      of the user's GPU. Written as\n\n     t = 1 - exp(-rate * dt);   x = lerp(x,
      target, t);          // RIGHT\n\n it does not. The two look nearly identical
      at any single framerate, which is exactly why the\n wrong one survives code
      review, and why this needs to be a permanent gate rather than a thing\n someone
      notices once.\n\n The wrong form also has a second, nastier property: `saturate`
      clamps at dt*speed >= 1, so a\n blend with speed 40 stops blending AT ALL below
      40 fps -- it snaps, every frame, and the\n smoothing silently ceases to exist
      exactly when the framerate is bad enough to need it most.\n\n ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n
      HOW Invariance() COMPARES, AND WHY IT IS FUSSY ABOUT IT:\n\n The obvious implementation
      -- resample every curve onto a fine common grid and diff them --\n FAILS THE
      CORRECT BLENDS. Interpolating a curve across a corner (MoveTowards saturating
      at 1.0,\n say) manufactures an error that has nothing to do with framerate,
      and the test then reports the\n good code as broken. That happened on the first
      attempt, and a test that cries wolf on correct\n code is worse than no test:
      it gets muted.\n\n So Invariance() compares two rates ONLY at sample times
      they genuinely share. For rates fa and\n fb, both have a sample at every multiple
      of 1/gcd(fa,fb) seconds. 72 and 90 coincide at 18 Hz;\n 120 and 144 at 24 Hz.
      Exact indices, zero interpolation, zero manufactured error.\n ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------"
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
