  - TypeFullName: Basis.IK.Motion.BasisMotionSignal
    MemberName: BasisMotionSignal
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 1
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Signal maths for the IK motion-quality harness: derivatives, zero-phase
      filtering, and the\n split of a joint path into \"what the body meant to do\"
      and \"what buzzes on top of it\".\n\n Lives in the RUNTIME assembly behind
      UNITY_EDITOR, like BasisBvhLoader, because both the\n NUnit suite (Basis.Framework.IK.Tests)
      and the editor sweeps (BasisEditor) must reach it and\n the tests asmdef does
      not reference BasisEditor. Putting it editor-side would force the tests\n to
      hand-mirror the maths, and a mirrored metric drifts.\n\n TWO TRAPS ARE BAKED
      INTO THIS FILE. Both were hit while calibrating the gates, and both\n silently
      produce a metric that lies:\n\n 1. NEVER DIFFERENTIATE A RAW SIGNAL. Jerk is
      a third derivative, so it amplifies frequency f\n    by (2*pi*f)^3. A 1 mm
      wobble at 30 Hz -- below the mocap's own optical noise floor and\n    invisible
      to the eye -- contributes 0.001 * (2*pi*30)^3 = 6700 m/s^3 of \"jerk\". Measured\n   
      raw, a real human elbow scores 23100; measured through a 6 Hz low-pass, it
      scores 1171.\n    The first number is the instrument. The second is the arm.
      Always LowPass() first.\n\n 2. A ZERO-PHASE FILTER HAS A STARTUP TRANSIENT
      THAT WILL EAT YOUR RMS. Filtering the CMU\n    corpus and taking the residual
      over the whole window reported 30 mm of \"human jitter\";\n    the first 8%
      of the clip alone read 108 mm while the middle 84% read 1.5 mm. The 1.5 mm
      is\n    the truth. LowPass() therefore odd-extends the signal before filtering
      (which preserves\n    the trend across the boundary -- a limb in motion has
      a big one) so the transient lives in\n    the padding and is discarded. Do
      not replace it with a plain zero-pad."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
