  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisArmBigSwingFlipTests
    MemberName: BasisArmBigSwingFlipTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\"Doing big arm swing motions (like up down for example) it flips drastically.\"\n\n
      The whole suite gated flips by WORKSPACE AREA (fraction of random poses over
      an elbow-gain\n threshold) and the flip passed every one of those gates, because
      a flip is not an area -- it is\n a SURFACE a moving hand crosses. A big up-down
      swing is a meridian path, so these tests sweep\n MERIDIANS: every azimuth around
      the shoulder, elevation +80 to -80, through the LIVE hint entry\n (BasisSwivelHintCore.ArmHint
      -- frame, mirror, field model, tuck), the same call SolveHand makes.\n\n WHAT
      THE FLIP WAS. BasisElbowFieldModel used to fade its projected bend toward a
      fixed rest pole\n below a 0.10 lever: normalizesafe(primary*w + rest*(1-w),
      rest). A lerp of two unit directions\n passes through ZERO where they are antipodal
      and w crosses 0.5 -- normalize(~0) -- and that\n cancellation surface cut through
      HEALTHY workspace (lever 0.05-0.07): hand-across-the-body at\n 0.45 reach teleported
      the elbow 28.7 cm in one 0.5-degree hand step; the down-back follow-through\n
      of a swing at 0.65 reach did the same at 108 degrees of hint rotation. Deterministic,
      no noise\n involved: it is math, and these sweeps reproduce it on the old code
      and pin it dead on the new.\n\n WHAT CANNOT BE PINNED. The bend is a tangent
      field on the sphere of hand directions, so by\n Poincare-Hopf it has total
      index 2: TWO zero cores per reach shell (across-body-up at ~0.75\n reach, down-back
      at ~0.65). Near a core the direction spins fast -- that is topology, not a
      bug,\n and no stateless formula removes it (the deleted fade just traded it
      for the teleport surface\n above). So the contract is: WHEREVER THE MODEL HAS
      A REAL LEVER (conditioning >= 0.02, which is\n everywhere outside ~2-degree
      cones around the two cores), a 0.5-degree hand step may not rotate\n the hint
      more than 30 degrees. The old fade fails this at 73 degrees; the projection
      passes with\n a worst of ~25."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisArmBigSwingFlipTests
    MemberName: BigSwings_NeverTeleportTheElbow_WhereTheModelHasALever
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 1
    ParamNames:
    - Frame(
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\u2B50\u2B50 THE SWING. Every meridian, three reaches, both arms. Wherever
      the model's lever is real,\n no single hand step may rotate the hint by more
      than 30 degrees. This is the test that was\n missing: on the faded model it
      fails at az -90 (hand across the body), reach 0.45, elevation\n ~37 -- hint
      rotating 73 degrees in one 0.5-degree step with a HEALTHY 0.057 lever."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
