  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisArmForearmRollAndSeamTests
    MemberName: BasisArmForearmRollAndSeamTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "TWO DEFECTS THAT NEED NO HUMAN-MOTION REFERENCE, WHICH IS EXACTLY WHY
      THEY ARE GATED HERE.\n\n This file deliberately asserts nothing about what
      a human arm does. An earlier gate in this\n investigation pinned a phantom-twist
      target against a RIGIDLY ABDUCTED reference arm, and the CMU\n corpus then
      showed real humans roll the humerus 40-70 deg through the same elevation range
      while the\n rigid reference rolls 6 -- so the reference, not the solver, was
      the outlier, and that gate would have\n pinned an artifact permanently. It
      was deleted. Everything below is a statement about the SOLVER'S OWN\n CONSISTENCY,
      which no corpus can overturn:\n\n   1. THE FOREARM'S AXIAL ROLL MUST NOT BE
      INHERITED FROM THE ANIMATION CLIP. Twisting the animated\n      forearm about
      its own long axis moves no bone position at all -- the IK problem handed to
      the\n      solver is byte-identical -- so the solved forearm must not move
      either. It used to track 1:1.\n\n   2. A SMOOTH INPUT MUST NOT PRODUCE 4x AMPLIFIED
      BONE ROLL. The wrist-roll relief's old wrap fade\n      crushed a capped 70
      deg of relief to zero over 23 deg of measured twist, so rolling the\n     
      controller one degree rolled the humerus up to 4.56. Whatever the right pose
      is, that is not it.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      \u26A0\uFE0F HOW THIS FILE AVOIDS BasisArmWristRollReliefTests' BLIND SPOT,
      WHICH IS STRUCTURAL AND NOT A\n CRITICISM OF THAT FILE -- IT MEASURES SOMETHING
      ELSE.\n\n That file's helpers build the controller rotation FROM THE ANIMATED
      BONES:\n     i.TipRotation    = i.MidRotation;\n     i.TargetRotation = Quaternion.AngleAxis(rollDeg,
      foreDir) * i.MidRotation;\n so the relief's `neutral` and the target are two
      expressions in the SAME animated forearm and the\n measured twist is IDENTICALLY
      the applied roll -- its own assertion says \"the measured roll IS the\n applied
      roll here\". Both defects above are invisible to that construction BY DEFINITION:
      defect 1 is a\n dependence ON the animated forearm (which is cancelled), and
      defect 2 lives at |twist| > 100 deg,\n which that construction can only reach
      by commanding a wrist roll no wrist can perform.\n\n SO NOTHING HERE DERIVES
      THE CONTROLLER ROTATION FROM AN ANIMATED BONE. The hand target rotation is\n
      built from the bind bone axis swung onto the SHOULDER->TARGET ray and then
      rolled, i.e. from the\n user's own geometry, and is therefore free to disagree
      with whatever the solver's neutral says --\n which is the whole quantity under
      test. Defect 1 goes further and holds the controller COMPLETELY\n FIXED while
      only the animated forearm's roll changes, so the input is provably constant.\n
      ================================================================================================"
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
