  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisArmHintExtensionSnapTests
    MemberName: BasisArmHintExtensionSnapTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "The elbow-hint snap at full extension (BasisArmSolveCore) \u2014 the
      arm's copy of the leg bug\n pinned by BasisLegHintExtensionSnapTests.\n\n The
      arm LOOKS like it already handles this: it computes a `hintFade` ramp. But
      that ramp is keyed on\n `ahProj`, the HINT's stand-off from the shoulder\u2192hand
      axis, which a real strapped-on elbow tracker\n keeps well clear of zero. The
      condition that actually admits the hint also demands\n `abProj.sqrMagnitude
      > totalLen^2 * 0.001` \u2014 and `abProj` is the ELBOW's own lever arm, which\n
      sweeps continuously to zero as the arm straightens. So the ramp fades a quantity
      that never collapses,\n while a boolean cliff sits on the one that does.\n\n
      The HintIsTracker floor makes it worse rather than better: it pins projNorm
      to 0.30, so `hintFade` is a\n flat 1.0 right up to the instant the abProj cliff
      trips. The elbow therefore falls from FULL hint to NONE\n in a single step
      \u2014 a bigger discontinuity than the leg's.\n\n Measured the same way: sweep
      the hand through the singularity and watch elbow travel per unit of hand\n
      travel. A pole switched off in one step teleports the elbow around the bend
      circle while the hand has\n barely moved. See BasisLegHintExtensionSnapTests
      for the full rationale."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
