    Summary: "CONTINUITY, SWEPT OVER EVERY INPUT THE ARM SOLVE HAS.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      THE CLASS THIS CATCHES: a SEAM -- a smooth input producing a jump in the output.
      The instance that\n shipped was a principal-angle clamp mapping +179.5 and
      -179.5 (the same humerus, one degree apart)\n onto +140 and -140 (poses 80
      degrees apart): an 80.009 degree arm jump for a 0.05 degree input step,\n 1430x
      amplification. A seam is INVISIBLE IN ANY SINGLE POSE by construction, so no
      per-pose suite of\n any size can find one -- only a sweep can, and only if
      it sweeps the input the seam lives on.\n\n So this file sweeps EVERY input,
      not the one that broke: hand direction (azimuth and elevation),\n reach through
      1.0, controller roll, elbow-tracker azimuth, tracker roll, and the animated
      pose's own\n roll -- and on every one it measures the worst single-step change
      of EVERY output: both bone\n rotations, both joint positions THROUGH THE STREAM,
      and each bone's AXIAL ROLL separately (a bone can\n swing smoothly while rolling
      discontinuously, and the roll is the DOF every singularity in this file\n pays
      out in).\n\n \u26A0\uFE0F THE HARNESS IS GATED BEFORE THE SOLVER IS. A sweep
      that builds its pole circle from a fixed world\n axis injects its own seam
      the moment the swept direction passes through that axis, and then reports\n
      the harness's discontinuity as the core's. Every sweep here therefore runs
      the same continuity gate\n over its INPUT channels first, at a far tighter
      bound, and a harness seam fails as a harness seam.\n\n The threshold is the
      sweep's OWN 95th-percentile step (see BasisArmNet.GateSmooth), not a constant:\n
      an absolute threshold has to be re-guessed for every unit and every step size,
      and guessing it wrong\n is how the 1430x amplifier passed review.\n ================================================================================================"
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
