  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.
    MemberName: ArmGuards_ReadTheTorsoUp_AndAreBlindToThePlayerRootUp
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 1
    ParamNames:
    - BasisArmNet.DefaultRig(
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\u2B50 WITH A TORSO FRAME SUPPLIED, THE ARM SOLVE MUST BE COMPLETELY
      BLIND TO THE PLAYER ROOT'S UP.\n\n This is the strongest possible form of the
      frame claim -- not \"close enough\", but BIT-IDENTICAL\n under an arbitrary
      re-orientation of the root up -- and it is cheap, because with a tracker hint\n
      and a bent animated arm PlayerUp has exactly one consumer left: the elbow anatomy
      guard's\n fallback, which TorsoUp displaces.\n\n \u26A0\uFE0F AND THE SECOND
      HALF IS WHAT MAKES THE FIRST HALF MEAN ANYTHING. With TorsoUp DECLINED (the\n
      struct default, which falls back to PlayerUp -- the pre-fix behaviour), the
      very same sweep MUST\n become sensitive to PlayerUp. Without that control,
      \"the result did not change\" would pass just\n as happily if the guard had
      stopped running at all."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
