  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisArmInvariantNetGuardReachabilityTests
    MemberName: BasisArmInvariantNetGuardReachabilityTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "EVERY GUARD MUST BE ABLE TO FIRE, AND MUST BE SHOWN TO FIRE, IN THE
      DOMAIN IT WAS WRITTEN FOR.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      THE CLASS THIS CATCHES: A GUARD THAT PROVABLY CANNOT FIRE. The instance was
      closed-form, not a\n tuning accident, and it was invisible for months behind
      a full suite of green tests:\n\n   BasisElbowAnatomyCore's only authority is
      a swivel about shoulder->hand, so the elbow it is asked\n   to move travels
      on a circle of radius rho about that axis. Over the whole circle and every
      hand\n   direction, max(elbow height - ceiling) == rho EXACTLY. So A SOFT MARGIN
      OF rho OR MORE IS A GUARD\n   THAT CAN NEVER FIRE -- not rarely, never.\n\n  
      SoftMarginFracLimb was 0.05 of the limb. At MaxElbowAngleDeg = 170 -- THE CAP,
      i.e. the\n   straightest arm this solver will ever produce, and where 41% of
      the corpus lives -- rho/L is\n   0.0436. Measured on the live core: 0 firings
      out of 22032 poses, with the elbow parked at the very\n   TOP of its circle,
      pointing at the sky. The guard existed, was correct, was tested, and was\n  
      arithmetically incapable of acting exactly where it was needed.\n\n The first
      test below is the CLOSED FORM version of that statement, and it takes no solver
      at all: it\n is one inequality over the two constants, checked at every elbow
      flexion the solver can produce,\n against the uncapped margin as its control.
      It would have caught the defect on the day the cap was\n introduced. The rest
      are empirical reachability sweeps, SEGMENTED rather than pooled -- pooling
      is\n how a 90 deg humeral twist limit passed review while breaching 3% in two
      separate elevation bands.\n ================================================================================================"
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
