  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.
    MemberName: ElbowAnatomyGuard_FiresAndBounds_InEveryExtensionBand
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 1
    ParamNames:
    - BasisArmNet.DefaultRig(
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "The elbow tracker is commanded to the TOP of the elbow circle -- the
      pose the guard exists to\n forbid -- across the whole extension range, and
      the guard must both FIRE and BOUND, in every band\n separately. Segmented,
      because pooling is how the last unreachable band hid.\n\n \u26A0\uFE0F THE
      CONTROL IS THE SAME SOLVE WITH THE GUARD SWITCHED OFF, not the commanded hint
      position.\n Zeroing BOTH ups makes BasisElbowAnatomyCore decline outright (`!(upSqr
      > k_SqrEpsilon)`) while\n changing nothing else on the tracker path -- PlayerUp's
      only other consumers are the\n straight-arm bend fallback and the pole-collapse
      stabiliser, and neither runs here. So the\n difference between the two solves
      is the guard and nothing but the guard. Reading the commanded\n hint instead
      would be wrong at high extension, where the tracker's own lever arm has collapsed\n
      further than the solved elbow's: the elbow the guard actually has to pull down
      is the one the\n SOLVE produced, not the one the tracker asked for.\n\n The
      bound is the guard's own rational saturation, evaluated here independently
      of the core:\n out = soft + M*e/(M + e), asymptotic to hard, with both margins
      capped at a fraction of the\n circle exactly as the core caps them."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
