  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisArmInvariantNetRollEnvelopeTests
    MemberName: BasisArmInvariantNetRollEnvelopeTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "THE AXIAL ROLL CHAIN: CLAVICLE -> HUMERUS -> FOREARM -> HAND. EVERY
      LINK, BOUNDED, SEPARATELY.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      THE CLASS THIS CATCHES: AN UNBOUNDED DEGREE OF FREEDOM. Humeral axial rotation
      was bounded by\n NOTHING -- measured in Unity, rolling an elbow tracker rolled
      the humerus 1:1 through a full 360\n inside a clavicle that never moved, because
      BasisShoulderSolveCore is direction-driven and a pure\n roll changes no direction,
      so the girdle contributed exactly 0.0 deg. Every neighbouring DOF was\n bounded
      -- elbow height, elbow flexion, forearm roll, wrist relief, clavicle-vs-chest
      -- and nobody\n noticed that one was not, because NOTHING ASSERTED THE TOTAL.\n\n
      The lesson generalises past the humerus: the way an unbounded DOF hides is
      that each neighbouring\n bound is asserted on its own, so the chain is never
      added up. This file adds it up. It measures each\n link independently, in the
      rig's OWN bind relationship (so it is a property of the skeleton, not of\n
      whatever animation clip is playing), and it holds the sum to a human total.\n\n
      THE HUMAN NUMBERS, and where each comes from:\n   humerus vs clavicle ....
      150 deg, this repo's own HumeralTwistHardDeg -- deliberately looser than a\n                           
      true glenohumeral arc (clinically ~48/71/40 at 45/90/135 of abduction)\n                           
      because this rig's girdle does not move, so the humerus is absorbing the\n                           
      scapula's share as well.\n   forearm vs humerus ..... ~85 deg each way. Pronation/supination
      is a FOREARM motion (the radius\n                            crossing the ulna);
      this repo calls the comfort band 80.\n   hand vs forearm ........ ~0. THE WRIST
      HAS NO AXIAL DEGREE OF FREEDOM. Radiocarpal motion is\n                           
      flexion/extension and radial/ulnar deviation; what feels like \"twisting\n                           
      your wrist\" is the forearm. A generous 15 deg is allowed here for soft\n                           
      tissue and rig slop.\n ================================================================================================"
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
