  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisAutoHeightModeTests
    MemberName: BasisAutoHeightModeTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Regression tests for the Auto height-mode decision\n (BasisCalibrationMath.AutoHeightModePicksArmSpan):
      trust the LONGER of the\n player's two body measurements. Both metrics under-measure
      easily (calibrating while seated\n or slouched reads the eye height 25-35%
      short; bent arms read the span short) but neither\n can over-measure past the
      real body \u2014 so the larger implied body height is the trustworthy\n measurement.
      Eye height is preferred inside a tolerance band (stabler, carries the\n standing-eye
      corrections); the span wins only when the eye reading is implausibly short\n
      against the measured reach.\n\n The field report that fixed the first (avatar-ratio-based)
      version of this rule: calibrated\n sitting in a chair with arms out \u2014
      eye read ~1.2 m while the span read ~1.7 m, and Auto took\n the broken eye
      height. That scenario is the headline case below."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
