  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.
    MemberName: RealHumans_StandingStill_MeasureAsBalanced
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 1
    ParamNames:
    - worstClips"
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\u2B50\u2B50 THE MOST IMPORTANT TEST IN THIS FILE. If a real human,
      measured standing still, fails this\n balance metric, then the METRIC is broken
      -- not the human. Everything else here is downstream of\n that claim, so it
      is asserted rather than assumed.\n\n Only QUASI-STATIC frames are eligible
      (CoM speed below 0.05 sqrt(gL), roughly 0.14 m/s). That is not\n a convenience:
      walking is a controlled fall and the CoM is SUPPOSED to leave the base of support\n
      during single support, so applying the static criterion to a walk cycle would
      fail every real human\n on physics rather than on error. The dynamic criterion
      covers those frames and is reported in test 5.\n\n MEASURED (twin, whole corpus):\n    
      per-clip median quasi-static margin, tier median:  root +0.346, posture +0.307
      foot lengths\n     per-clip balanced fraction, tier median:           root 
      1.000, posture  0.973\n     quasi-static frames available:                    
      root  5420, posture  8586\n The asserted floors are +0.10 and 0.85, i.e. 3.1x
      and 1.14x headroom on the worse tier. A real human\n standing still keeps their
      centre of mass about a THIRD OF A FOOT LENGTH inside their own footprint,\n
      which is both a sane-looking number and the one this metric has to reproduce
      to be trusted.\n\n Tier MEDIANS, not per-clip: the corpus contains sits, a
      lying-down clip and airborne clips, whose\n real base of support includes furniture
      and floor this layer cannot see. Those are named in the\n class header and
      printed below rather than filtered away."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
