  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.
    MemberName: TheMargin_DoesNotCareWhereTheHumanStands_OrHowBigTheyAre
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 1
    ParamNames:
    - delta"
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "The other half of the proof, and the same shape as BasisElbowAnatomyTests'
      \"every possible pose\n passes through untouched\": a metric that moves when
      nothing about the posture changed is reading\n something other than the posture.\n\n
      WHERE the human stands is not balance. The margin is a distance from the CoM
      to the edge of the\n human's own feet, so translating the whole body across
      the floor must leave it EXACTLY unchanged --\n asserted at 1e-4, measured at
      1e-15 (i.e. floating-point noise; there is nothing absolute in the\n computation
      at all).\n\n HOW BIG the human is is not balance either -- but stating that
      correctly takes care. The margin is\n divided by foot length so a child avatar
      and a giant get the same number for the same posture, the\n same scale-free
      convention BasisMocapFootQuality uses. The scaling that must leave it EXACTLY\n
      unchanged is FROUDE scaling: lengths by k and time by sqrt(k), which is the
      similarity that\n preserves every dimensionless quantity in the file (v/sqrt(gL),
      heights over leg length, the lot).\n Measured worst case over every clip this
      test selects: 2.7e-15, i.e. floating-point noise.\n\n \u26A0 SCALING SPACE
      ALONE IS NOT INVARIANT, AND MUST NOT BE MADE SO. Blow the skeleton up 1.7x
      while\n leaving the frame rate alone and the margin shifts by up to 2.05e-2
      (measured on 143_18). That is\n CORRECT: a giant moving at the same metres
      per second really is moving more slowly in body lengths,\n so v/sqrt(gL) changes,
      so a different set of frames qualifies as quasi-static and the median is\n
      taken over a different population. An earlier version of this test asserted
      space-only invariance\n within 0.01 and failed on 3 of 9 clips. The fix was
      to state the right invariance, not to widen the\n tolerance until the wrong
      one passed."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
