  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisBlendSpeedTests
    MemberName: BasisBlendSpeedTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 1
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\"Is it blending at the CORRECT SPEED\" -- gates every temporal blend
      in the IK stack against the\n one property that makes the question answerable:
      a blend's speed must not depend on the user's\n framerate.\n\n Basis ships
      to 72 Hz (Quest, Steam Frame), 80, 90 (Index, Pico), 120 and 144 Hz headsets,
      and to\n desktop at whatever the monitor does. A smoother written as\n\n    
      t = saturate(dt * speed);   x = lerp(x, target, t);          // WRONG\n\n has
      a time constant that is a function of the GPU. Written as `1 - exp(-rate*dt)`
      it does not.\n The two look nearly identical at any ONE framerate -- which
      is exactly why the wrong form passes\n review, and why this has to be a standing
      gate instead of something someone notices once.\n\n TWO REAL BUGS ARE PINNED
      HERE. Both were live in this repo, and both are fixed in the same commit\n
      as this file:\n\n   1. BasisVirtualSpineCore.SmoothSlerpBurst  -- neck, chest,
      spine and hips all used\n      saturate(dt*speed). Perverse fingerprint: because
      a smaller dt gives a smaller alpha gives a\n      SLOWER filter, a 144 Hz headset
      got ~60% MORE neck lag than a 72 Hz one from the same\n      setting. And `saturate`
      clamps at dt*speed >= 1, so with the default NeckRotationSpeed of 40\n     
      the smoothing VANISHED below 40 fps: the neck snapped every frame, precisely
      when the\n      framerate was bad enough to need smoothing most.\n\n   2. BasisFootSimulateJob's
      swing branch -- `slerp(currentRot, liveRot, ease)` fed its own output\n     
      back in, so the foot's rotation converged by FRAME COUNT rather than elapsed
      time. Mid-swing\n      through an identical 0.35 s step the foot was 74% rotated
      at 30 fps and 99.7% at 144 Hz --\n      literally a different pose on a faster
      GPU. It hid because the ENDPOINT always converges\n      (ease reaches 1),
      so the error only existed while the foot was moving.\n\n House rule followed
      here: every gate that asserts the fixed form is correct is PAIRED with one\n
      that drives the OLD form and asserts it FAILS. Without the pair, a bug that
      made the invariance\n metric always return zero would leave every test green
      and the gate silently dead."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
