    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.
    MemberName: VirtualSpine_LagDoesNotGrowWithFramerate_BeyondTheSamplingFloor
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 1
    ParamNames:
    - alpha
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "The fingerprint that made the original bug so hard to believe: with
      saturate(dt*speed), the BETTER\n your headset, the LAGGIER your neck.\n\n \u26A0
      YOU CANNOT ASSERT THAT LAG IS IDENTICAL ACROSS FRAMERATES. It is not, it cannot
      be, and demanding\n it is how the first version of this test failed the CORRECT
      code.\n\n A discrete one-pole's phase lag is tau - dt/2. That half-frame is
      the SAMPLER, not the filter: the\n filter reacts to the current sample immediately,
      so a coarser clock actually shows LESS phase lag in\n milliseconds, converging
      up to tau as dt shrinks. No implementation removes it. Between 72 and 144 Hz\n
      the irreducible floor is (1/72 - 1/144)/2 = 3.47 ms.\n\n So the honest claims
      are:\n   * the FIX's lag difference is explained ENTIRELY by that sampling
      floor (measured 2.84 ms), and\n   * the LEGACY form's is far bigger (measured
      6.88 ms, 2.4x), because its underlying TIME CONSTANT\n     genuinely changes
      with framerate -- 17.1 ms at 72 Hz, 21.3 ms at 144 Hz.\n\n The framerate-independent
      quantity is the TIME CONSTANT, not the phase lag. That is what\n VirtualSpine_EveryRotationBlend_RunsAtTheSameSpeedOnEveryHeadset
      asserts, and it is the real gate."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
