  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisBodyPlausibilityTests
    MemberName: BasisBodyPlausibilityTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "IS THE WHOLE BODY A BODY? -- the two whole-skeleton checks, and, first,
      the proof that they can\n measure anything at all.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      GUARD THE RULER BEFORE YOU USE IT. This project has shipped metrics that lied:\n\n  
      * a pop detector whose count came out IDENTICAL whether its cause was present
      or absent;\n   * a smoothness metric that scored over-smoothed mush ABOVE a
      real human;\n   * an elbow-guard test that reported safety while the bug it
      was written for was still live,\n     because it asserted on a pose the geometry
      could not produce (BasisElbowAnatomyTests now\n     carries RequireInReach
      for exactly that reason).\n\n So nothing here reports a number until the number
      has been checked against something already known.\n The order of this file
      is deliberate and it is the order the tests must be read in:\n\n   1. THE ANSWER
      KEYS. Two channels have published values measured by somebody else:\n     
      BasisElbowAnatomyCore's +0.015 arm lengths over 55,140 frames, and BasisSpineAnatomy's
      p99\n      table over 35,081. If this file cannot reproduce those, it is wrong
      and nothing below counts.\n\n      \u26A0 AND ONE OF THEM DID NOT REPRODUCE
      -- WHICH IS THE POINT, AND IT HAS SINCE BEEN ACTED ON. Run\n      2026-07-22:
      the spine table reproduced (and its 35,081 is exactly this corpus's root+posture\n     
      frame count, which is its own corroboration). The ELBOW law reproduced ONLY
      when measured in a\n      torso-relative frame -- in the frame the shipped
      guard enforced AT THE TIME (PlayerUp, world up)\n      real humans exceeded
      its soft margin, on the very population the published number was taken\n     
      over. That was a defect in shipped code, not in the ruler, and this file said
      so rather than\n      widening a margin to make the disagreement go away. BasisArmSolveCore
      now passes a torso up, and\n      the evidence trail is in TheRuler_ReproducesTheElbowCeilingLaw's
      header.\n   2. THE DETECTOR CAN FIRE. A self-intersection check that never
      fires is indistinguishable from a\n      clean body. So drive a synthetic arm
      through a synthetic chest and demand it be caught.\n   3. REAL HUMANS PASS.
      Only now is it meaningful that the corpus comes back clean.\n      \u26A0 ON
      13 OF THE 25 CAPSULE PAIRS FOR DEPTH. The other 12 (arm x same-side leg) are
      rate-checked\n      only: their depth number saturates on real motion, measured,
      and the exclusion is itself\n      asserted so it cannot outlive its evidence.
      See RealHumans_DoNotPassThroughThemselves.\n   4. THE REPORT. The measured
      envelope, per channel, per corpus tier. No gate -- the numbers do not\n     
      exist yet, and there is no honest threshold before that. THE RATCHET SITES
      ARE MARKED.\n\n TIERS ARE LOADED NON-RECURSIVELY, ONE AT A TIME, and reported
      as separate rows. The corpus is split\n deliberately (root 20 / posture 44
      / dynamic 29 / slow 16) so one tier cannot re-base another's\n numbers -- the
      accuracy harness's headline figures are quoted all over this project and they
      stay\n byte-identical only because nobody folds the tiers together. For ROM
      the tier that matters is\n dynamic/: arms overhead, full extension, fast swings,
      martial arts. That is where the extremes are.\n ================================================================================================"
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
