  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.
    MemberName: RealHumans_DoNotPassThroughThemselves
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\u2B50 THE RULER-GUARD FOR (A). Real humans do not pass through themselves.
      If this fires, THE\n DETECTOR IS WRONG -- the radii are too fat, or a pair
      that should have been excluded as adjacent\n was not. Do not \"fix\" it by
      relaxing the threshold: the whole value of this layer is that a hit\n means
      something.\n\n It is a RATE, not a zero, and that follows the house precedent\n
      (BasisSpineAnatomyCorpusTests.k_MaxFireFraction = 3%): a corpus of real motion
      contains retarget\n slop and genuine limb-on-limb contact, and demanding literal
      zero would make the test a\n tuning-fork for the corpus rather than a check
      on the code. The per-pair breakdown is printed so\n a failure names the pair
      immediately instead of sending someone hunting.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      \u26A0 DEPTH IS ASSERTED ON 13 OF THE 25 PAIRS, NOT ALL 25, AND THE 12 EXCLUSIONS
      WERE MEASURED, NOT\n ASSUMED. The arm x same-side-leg family saturates its
      depth number on real human motion: measured\n 2026-07-22 over 109 clips / 133,078
      frames, those pairs reach 0.946-0.999 of their SUMMED CORE\n RADII (bone axes
      effectively coincident -- dynamic/135_01 f2377 has them 0.0005 m apart), while\n
      every other pair in the corpus stays at or below 0.448. The split is bimodal
      with nothing in\n between, and the deep hits are interior to both segments
      in smooth multi-frame arcs, so they are\n real sustained motion rather than
      glitch frames -- CMU is solved without collision constraints.\n `fracLimb`
      also has a hard per-pair ceiling of (rA+rB)/min(lenA,lenB), which for forearm
      x thigh\n is only ~0.25, so the 0.261 that failed this test on 2026-07-22 WAS
      that pair's ceiling: the\n threshold and the saturation point were the same
      number, and no value could have separated\n contact from pass-through there.
      The full reasoning lives next to `Pair` in BasisBodyPlausibility.\n\n Those
      12 pairs keep their RATE assertion (a solver putting an arm through a leg on
      5% of frames\n is still caught) and the exclusion itself is CHECKED below,
      so it cannot outlive its evidence.\n ================================================================================================"
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
