  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.
    MemberName: MeasuredEnvelope_PerChannel_PerTier_TheRatchetSource
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 1
    ParamNames:
    - int>(
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\u2B50 THE MEASURED ENVELOPE, per channel, per corpus tier. THIS IS
      THE DELIVERABLE.\n\n Nothing is asserted about the ungated channels because
      nothing can honestly be: their limits are\n clinical max-voluntary ROM and
      have never met this corpus through this code. What the table\n answers is the
      only question that matters before gating one:\n\n     DOES THE LIMIT SIT OUTSIDE
      WHAT REAL HUMANS ACTUALLY DID?\n\n If a channel's p99 is comfortably inside
      its envelope, the limit is safe to gate -- flip\n BasisRomSpec.Gated to true
      in BasisBodyPlausibility.BuildSpecs. If p99 is OUTSIDE\n it, the limit is WRONG
      and gating it would clip real motion; widen it to the literature's upper\n
      bound, or accept that CMU retargeting slop is producing poses the subject never
      made and say so.\n\n TIERS ARE SEPARATE ROWS ON PURPOSE. dynamic/ is where
      the extremes live -- arms overhead, full\n extension, martial arts -- so a
      limit that survives root/ and dies in dynamic/ is a limit fitted\n to people
      walking around a lab, which is exactly the failure BasisSpineAnatomy's header
      warns\n about. Reading them pooled would hide it."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
