  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisBodyProportionTests
    MemberName: BasisBodyProportionTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Body-proportion regression tests for the two-bone limb solvers.\n\n
      Every other IK test in this suite runs one \"adult-ish\" skeleton. These run
      HUMAN INPUT POSES\n (the targets a person actually produces in VR: hands at
      sides, touching the chin, reaching\n across to the other shoulder, squatting,
      sitting, stepping onto a box...) across a catalog of\n body proportions from
      a 0.6 m chibi to a 2.4 m giant, including skewed segment ratios\n (long-forearm,
      long-thigh) and width extremes (lanky vs stocky). Poses are built from BODY\n
      LANDMARKS (chin, opposite shoulder, knee, a step of absolute height), so the
      same human intent\n lands at a different reach ratio and direction on every
      body -- exactly what avatar variety\n does to the solver in production.\n\n
      Three failure classes are unique to proportion changes, and each gets a dedicated
      guard here:\n\n   1. ABSOLUTE-LENGTH ASSUMPTIONS -- a constant tuned in metres
      (a fade window, an epsilon, a\n      clearance) that is correct at 1.75 m and
      wrong at 0.6 m / 2.4 m. Guarded by\n      Solve_IsScaleInvariant_FromChibiToGiant:
      a geometrically-similar body must produce\n      identical angles at any scale.\n  
      2. MIRROR ASYMMETRY -- left/right limbs solved through mirrored inputs must
      give mirrored\n      outputs on EVERY body, or one arm looks natural and the
      other doesn't.\n   3. RATIO-TRIGGERED POLE FLIPS -- a pole/axis choice that
      is stable at 50:50 upper:lower but\n      flips somewhere along the ratio range
      rigs actually ship. Guarded by\n      Solve_StaysContinuous_AsLimbProportionsMorph.\n\n
      Thresholds were calibrated offline against a float64 port of the cores running
      this exact\n matrix (ik_bodyprop_calibrate.py); each assert notes the measured
      value so a failure means a\n real regression, not float noise."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
