  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisBoneChainLagTests
    MemberName: BasisBoneChainLagTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 1
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "The THIRD site of the saturate(dt*speed) bug \u2014 and the one that
      lands on the LEGS.\n\n BasisBlendSpeedTests already pins this bug class in
      the virtual spine and the foot swing. It was\n never fixed in BasisBoneSimChainJob,
      which is the job every bone WITHOUT a tracker role goes\n through. A physical
      tracker with no role assigned leaves HasTracked = HasNoTracker\n (BasisInput.SetRealTrackers),
      so that bone takes the job's target-follow branch and LERPS toward\n its target
      bone.\n\n And bone targets CHAIN: Hips -> UpperLeg -> LowerLeg -> Foot -> Toes
      (BasisLocalAvatarDriver:387).\n So a roleless leg is three cascaded lerps deep,
      while a TRACKED bone is a pure passthrough\n (trackersmooth = 25, and math.saturate(25)
      == 1). That asymmetry IS \"the legs lag behind the rest\n of the trackers\".\n\n
      The old form's fingerprint, measured here on the real job through a 60 deg
      turn:\n\n      30 fps  alpha 1.000  -> foot slides   0 ms   (smoothing OFF
      -- the leg SNAPS)\n      90 fps  alpha 0.444  -> foot slides  78 ms\n     240
      fps  alpha 0.167  -> foot slides 100 ms\n\n The better your headset, the laggier
      your legs; and below 40 fps the smoothing switched itself off\n entirely, exactly
      where the framerate was bad enough to need it most."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
