  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisButterflyKneeSweepTests
    MemberName: BasisButterflyKneeSweepTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\"Butterfly knees when laying down\" sweep for BasisButterflyKneeCore
      and its hand-off to the\n two-bone leg solver (BasisLegSolveCore).\n\n Scenario:
      lie on your back with FOOT trackers but no KNEE trackers, press the soles together
      (tilt the feet\n outward so the instep faces out) and pull them in toward the
      pelvis -- the knees should fall open laterally\n (the \"butterfly\"/cobbler
      pose). Foot tilt drives how far the knees open; pulling the feet in amplifies
      it;\n and the open angle is HARD-CLAMPED to the hip's natural max abduction
      (MaxOpenDeg) so the splay can't exceed\n what the hips physically allow.\n\n
      These are characterization + guard sweeps. A few just print tables; the asserted
      ones pin the contract:\n   - gated fully OFF when standing or when the feet
      are flat (no false splay during normal use),\n   - the open angle is monotonic
      in foot tilt and amplified by pull-in,\n   - it never exceeds MaxOpenDeg for
      ANY input (the clamp the user asked for),\n   - the knee actually swings OUTWARD
      through the real two-bone solver, finitely and bounded.\n The hint is fed to
      the leg solver exactly as BasisLocalRigDriver wires it (HintPosition + HintWeight,\n
      BendNormal = the hips' right axis = KneeBendPref), so the knee excursion is
      measured end-to-end."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
