  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisCervicalDirectionTests
    MemberName: BasisCervicalDirectionTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\"Chicken neck\" / \"gamer neck\" regression tests for the cervical-lordosis
      IK.\n\n These exercise the pure, stream-free neck math directly -- BasisCervicalSolveCore\n
      (the port of BasisFullIKConstraintJob.ApplyCervicalLordosis). It computes the
      forward neck/upper-\n chest curl from head-gaze pitch, the head-pitch clamp,
      and the extreme-region whole-body\n counterbalance. They guard the failure
      mode the neck has:\n\n   CHICKEN NECK -- the head poking forward/down with
      the neck craned, instead of sitting with a\n                   gentle resting
      lordosis that flexes naturally with gaze.\n\n The headline guards are: the
      RESTING forward curl at level gaze stays small (a regression that\n cranks
      the base bend is exactly the chicken-neck artifact), the curl is correctly
      SIGNED (looking\n down flexes forward, looking up extends back -- never the
      reverse), grows MONOTONICALLY with gaze,\n is CLAMPED so the gaze can't crane
      past the anatomical limit, and has NO RATE KINK at level gaze\n (the most common
      pose, where a hard abs() would snap the neck ~4x). The bounds are intentionally\n
      looser than the values the current solver produces (each test notes the measured
      headroom) so they\n fail only on a real regression, not on float noise -- and
      they bound the curl from ABOVE, so the\n neck can be *tuned down* (less chicken
      neck) without tripping them.\n\n This is the same math the offline BasisHeadSweep
      CSV harness scans, asserted here so the \"gentle\n forward lordosis, never
      a forward-poked chicken neck\" property is checked in CI instead of by\n eyeballing
      a CSV. Sign convention (verified against the sweep): a positive pitch command
      is looking\n DOWN, matching Quaternion.Euler(x,0,0)."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisChestTrackerAuthorityTests
    MemberName: BasisChestTrackerAuthorityTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 1
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\"THE HEAD DRAGS MY CHEST AROUND.\" Gates BasisFullIKConstraintJob.ReassertTrackedChest.\n\n
      A chest tracker is a MEASUREMENT. SolveSpine wrote it to the chest bone ONCE,
      before the solve, and\n then every stage after it overwrote it while chasing
      the HEAD: DistributeSpineBend writes the Spine\n (the chest's PARENT), BiasSpineTowardChest
      writes it again, and above all SolveSequentialSpineIK's\n CCD rotates the chest
      DIRECTLY at chain index chainLen-3 plus the Spine underneath it. Nothing pulled\n
      it back -- ClampChestCone bounds the chest against its PARENT and never against
      the tracker, and\n SolveChestTarget early-returns because chestIkTarget defaults
      false (and even switched on it pulls\n chest POSITION, never ROTATION). ReassertTrackedChest
      re-applies the measurement at the END of the\n solve and restores the head
      using only the joints ABOVE the chest.\n\n ==============================================================================================\n
      THE METRIC IS DEGREES OF CHEST PER DEGREE OF GAZE, WITH THE TRACKER HELD FIXED.\n\n
      Hips pinned (hips tracker on, so all three pelvis-synthesis stages are suppressed
      and the pelvis is\n the commanded target bit for bit), chest tracker fed and
      held at the frame's own measurement, and\n then ONLY the head moves -- orbited
      about the neck pivot, which is exactly what a user looking down\n does and
      exactly the input the solver receives. A real human's chest pitches -0.05 deg
      per degree of\n gaze, i.e. not at all, so zero is not an approximation of the
      right answer here, it IS the answer.\n\n NOT pooled spine-vs-mocap POSITION
      error. That ruler has repeatedly misled in this repo: it sums\n \"where the
      pelvis ended up\" with \"how the chain above it was bent\", it is dominated
      by the former,\n and it cannot see a rotation complaint at all -- switching
      chestIkTarget on moves chest POSITION\n 3.23 -> 0.29 cm while chest ROTATION
      goes 11.38 -> 11.51, i.e. the position ruler would have called\n that a fix.
      The quantity the user is complaining about is an ANGLE, so the gate is an angle.\n\n
      MEASURED (10-clip CMU corpus, chest tracker + hips tracker, shipped cone 90
      and anatomical ROM on):\n                                        before     
      after\n     chest deg per deg of GAZE           0.402      0.008\n     chest-vs-tracker
      rotation error    11.68 deg   1.45 deg\n     chest deg per m   of HAND           
      0.02      0.02      (already zero on this branch)\n ==============================================================================================\n\n
      \u26A0\uFE0F THE CONTROLS ARE NOT DECORATION. A rig that silently fails to
      move would pass every \"the chest\n must not move\" assertion in this file,
      so no threshold here is allowed to stand on its own:\n\n   * the GAZE gate
      is paired, in the same test, with the identical sweep run at HasChestTracker
      =\n     false, which must show a LARGE coupling (measured 0.390 deg/deg --
      essentially the pre-fix\n     number, because the pre-fix tracker branch was
      letting the CCD win anyway). One rig, one sweep,\n     one flag between them:
      if the harness were inert both would read zero and the test fails.\n   * the
      CHEST-VS-TRACKER gate is paired with the same measurement on the no-chest branch,
      where\n     nothing ever writes the measurement and the error must therefore
      be large.\n   * the NO-CHEST BRANCH IS ITSELF GATED. ApplyArmSwingChestFollow
      is by design a hand->chest\n     coupling and it lives on the else side of
      the dispatch; it must still measure ~27 deg/m. That\n     is what makes this
      change provably scoped to tracker users rather than a global chest freeze.\n  
      * the HEAD IS STILL PLACED, so the fix cannot be \"pin the chest and abandon
      the HMD\".\n\n RIG PROVENANCE: construction, corpus, gaze frame, chest frame
      and every solver parameter mirror\n BasisTrackerConfigMatrixTests bone for
      bone -- the one harness in this suite that drives the real\n public SolveSpine
      dispatch, so the chest branch actually executes. Two traps that harness documents\n
      and that this file inherits: MaxChestDeltaProperty MUST be set (production
      ships 90; unset means a\n zero-degree chest cone that nobody runs), and spineAnatomicalRom
      is only real once the envelope is\n BAKED (GuardSpineJoint also returns when
      ChainSpineRestFrames is not created)."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
