  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisElbowAnatomyReachTests
    MemberName: BasisElbowAnatomyReachTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 1
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "A GUARD WHOSE MARGIN IS BIGGER THAN ITS REACH IS NOT A GUARD. This
      file is the proof that this one's\n no longer is, and the proof that it once
      was.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      WHAT WENT WRONG, IN ONE LINE: BasisElbowAnatomyCore.SoftMarginFracLimb (0.05)
      was a FRACTION OF THE\n ARM, and BasisArmSolveCore.MaxElbowAngleDeg (170) caps
      how far the elbow can get from the arm axis\n (radius/L = 0.0436). Two constants
      in two files, no relation asserted between them, and the second\n silently
      ate the first.\n\n BasisElbowAnatomyTests next door proves the guard's MATHS
      -- unreachable illegal poses, byte-identical\n legal ones. It cannot see this
      defect, because every pose it asserts on is one where the guard was\n still
      alive. The bug lived in the poses it never asked about: the fully extended
      ones, which is where\n a VR user's arms actually are. 41.1% of the CMU corpus
      is past 0.95 extension and a user whose real\n arms are longer than their avatar's
      is past the cap ON EVERY FRAME.\n\n So this file asks a different question
      -- not \"is the guard correct\" but \"IS THE GUARD ALIVE\" -- and\n it asks
      it in the four places that can answer:\n\n   1. THE THEOREM. max(elbow height
      above the ceiling), over the whole circle and every hand\n      direction,
      is EXACTLY the circle's radius. So margin >= radius == a guard that cannot
      fire.\n   2. THE ANTI-TAUTOLOGY. The old rule, evaluated on the same grid at
      the extension cap, fires ZERO\n      times. If that assertion ever stops holding,
      this file's headline is stale and must be re-read.\n   3. THE COUPLING CANNOT
      COME BACK. The guard must stay live for EVERY MaxElbowAngleDeg anyone might\n     
      plausibly set, not just today's 170.\n   4. REAL HUMANS ARE STILL NOT CLIPPED
      -- segmented by extension band, because pooling hides\n      breaches (a 90
      deg humeral twist limit passed pooled and breached 3% in two bands once split).\n\n
      \u26A0 AND ONE THING THIS FILE DELIBERATELY REFUSES TO DO: size anything off
      the top extension band's pole\n statistics. See TheCorpusCannotRefereeThePoleAtFullExtension,
      which asserts the blind spot rather\n than merely mentioning it, so a future
      revision cannot fit a constant to noise without this failing.\n ================================================================================================"
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
