  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.
    MemberName: TheFallback_ReproducesTheOldWorldFrameBehaviour_WhenNoTorsoFrameIsAvailable
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\u2B50 THE REGRESSION TEST PROPER: THE SAME SOLVE, TWICE, DIFFERING
      ONLY IN TorsoUp. This is the one\n that goes red if the fix is reverted, and
      it pins BOTH halves of it at once.\n\n     TorsoUp SET  -> the solve is the
      exact identity. Measured: the elbow moves 0.000000 cm.\n     TorsoUp ZERO ->
      BasisArmSolveCore falls back to i.PlayerUp, WHICH IS THE PRE-FIX BEHAVIOUR,\n                    
      and the guard fires: -41.515 deg, hauling the elbow 15.04 cm off a pose that\n                    
      was never wrong. (That is the median-trunk figure; at the top of the measured\n                    
      band, 119.7 deg, the same pose costs 61.82 deg and 21.80 cm -- which is the\n                    
      corpus's own 66.15 deg / 21.28 cm worst case, reproduced synthetically.)\n\n
      THE FALLBACK IS DELIBERATE AND IT IS ALSO THE DEFECT, so it is pinned rather
      than tolerated. It\n is reject-unless-good: a caller that cannot build a body
      frame gets a guarded arm rather than no\n guard at all, and for an upright
      torso it is the same vector anyway. But it must not silently\n rot into the
      only path, so this asserts it still reproduces the OLD behaviour, at the size
      that\n behaviour misfires by.\n\n VERIFIED BY REVERTING, 2026-07-22: with `guardUp`
      forced back to `i.PlayerUp`, the first\n assertion below fails with the elbow
      moved 15.036346 cm, and the second still passes -- i.e. the\n fallback genuinely
      is the old behaviour and this test genuinely distinguishes them.\n\n THE SOLVE
      IS THE IDENTITY BY CONSTRUCTION, so the guard is the only thing that can move
      the\n elbow and the measurement is unambiguous: no hint (so neither the hint
      swivel nor the\n pole-collapse stabilizer runs), target == hand (so the triangle
      solve and the root rotation are\n both no-ops), and TipRotation/HintRotation
      left at zero (so no wrist relief and no forearm roll)."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.
    MemberName: AnIllegalPoseInTheTiltedFrame_IsStillCaught_TheFixReAimsTheGuardItDoesNotDisableIt
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 4
    ParamNames:
    - height")
    - shoulder")
    - shoulder")
    - height"
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\u2B50 THE FIX RE-AIMS THE GUARD; IT DOES NOT DISABLE IT. The cheapest
      way to make\n ABentTorso_LeavesEveryLegalPoseUntouched pass would be to stop
      guarding, so this demands the\n opposite half: the CHICKEN WING -- the reported
      bug, an elbow driven to the top of its circle --\n must still be caught when
      the body is bent double, and caught IN THE TILTED FRAME.\n\n Same rigid-rotation
      fixture as the legal case, so each pose is illegal against the tilted chest\n
      by exactly the margin AnElbowPointedAtTheSky_IsPulledBackUnderTheCeiling establishes
      upright.\n The reachable-AND-illegal precondition is re-asserted at every tilt
      for the reason RequireInReach\n exists: a guard test that starts from a pose
      the geometry cannot produce reports safety while the\n bug is live, and this
      file has been burned by that once already.\n\n HEADROOM: 30 combinations (6
      tilts x 5 wings). Every one fires, every one lands under the tilted\n hard
      limit (ceiling + 0.15 arm lengths), and every one keeps both bone lengths to
      within 1e-3."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
