  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisElbowDirectionTests
    MemberName: BasisElbowDirectionTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Elbow-direction regression tests for the arm IK sweep system.\n\n These
      exercise the pure, stream-free elbow math directly -- BasisArmBendLookup\n
      (the chest-relative bend-direction lookup) and BasisArmSolveCore (the two-bone\n
      solve, a fork of Unity's SolveTwoBoneIK with the Basis pole-flip / rate-limit
      handling). They\n guard the failure modes the elbow used to have:\n\n   1.
      ELBOW FLIP   -- the pole snapping to the opposite side on smooth motion / tracker
      noise.\n   2. ELBOW IN FRONT -- the elbow winging up and forward instead of
      tucking naturally behind\n                        and below the hand.\n   3.
      FULL-EXTENSION FLIP -- as the arm extends out fully the elbow pole collapses
      onto the\n                        shoulder->hand axis; the elbow must ride
      along, not snap to the wrong side.\n\n Thresholds are intentionally looser
      than the values the current solver produces (each test\n notes the measured
      headroom) so they fail only on a real regression, not on float noise.\n\n This
      is the same geometry the offline BasisArmIKSweep CSV harness scans, asserted
      here so the\n \"elbow behind the hand, never flipped\" property is checked
      in CI instead of by eyeballing a CSV."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisElbowDownAtExtensionTests
    MemberName: BasisElbowDownAtExtensionTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "A straight arm must not carry a large permanent HUMERAL ROLL.\n\n THE
      DEFECT (user: \"i do a tpose while in vr with my arms and it rotates\", and
      the same in a starfish).\n BasisElbowFieldModel predicts the CORPUS-MEAN elbow,
      and CMU contains essentially no straight-arm lateral\n holds -- a T-pose is
      a calibration pose, not a motion -- so the mean there is dragged backward by
      the\n reaching motions that DO trail the elbow. Measured on the shipping model:
      at a lateral straight arm it\n wanted the elbow 52 deg BACK of straight down,
      where a real one hangs down.\n\n WHY THAT IS EXPENSIVE. At full extension the
      elbow's lever arm is ~2 cm, so 52 deg of azimuth buys almost\n no elbow DISPLACEMENT
      -- it is paid almost entirely as humeral ROLL. Roll moves no joint, so every\n
      position gate in this repo is structurally blind to it (the documented blind
      spot), while it is very\n visible as a twisted, pinched deltoid. Measured 53
      deg of permanent twist on an elbow-down bind.\n\n TWO EXPLANATIONS WERE RULED
      OUT BY MEASUREMENT BEFORE THE FIX, and the tests below keep them ruled out:\n  
      - NOT a conditioning collapse: d(roll)/d(hand) was 0.15 deg/mm at every reach
      INCLUDING 1.000, i.e.\n     stable, not noise. A singularity fix would have
      been the wrong tool.\n   - EXACTLY a fixed offset: roll tracked the avatar's
      BIND azimuth at slope -1.00, so it is the constant\n     disagreement between
      the bind and the model's preferred azimuth."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
