  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisElbowFieldTests
    MemberName: BasisElbowFieldTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: " <summary>\n Guards <see cref=\"BasisElbowFieldModel\"/> -- where the
      elbow goes with no elbow tracker.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      THE BUG THESE TESTS EXIST TO STOP COMING BACK.\n\n Its predecessor predicted
      the elbow's SWIVEL ANGLE. An angle has to be measured FROM something, and\n
      the reference it chose was body-DOWN, projected perpendicular to the arm:\n\n    
      u = normalize(down - axis * dot(down, axis))          |u| = sin(arm's angle
      off vertical)\n\n which is ZERO when the hand is directly below the shoulder.
      Measured on the 55,140-frame corpus,\n |u| < 0.2 on 29.7% OF REAL HUMAN FRAMES,
      minimum 0.001. On nearly a third of poses the swivel was\n measured against
      a direction that did not exist, and normalizesafe then snapped it to a fallback.\n\n
      The user's report was \"the elbows flip around\". Instrumented, the shipped
      model did this: the hand\n sways 3 cm fore-aft under the shoulder -- standing
      still, arms relaxed, the commonest pose there is\n in VR -- and the elbow swung
      49 degrees and travelled 19.6 cm.\n\n AND NO REFIT COULD HAVE SAVED IT. The
      pole is a unit tangent vector on the sphere of hand\n directions, and by the
      HAIRY BALL THEOREM every continuous such field vanishes somewhere. The\n singularity
      was never removable, only movable -- and it had been moved onto the rest pose.\n\n
      A POSITION carries no such obstruction. This model predicts the elbow's position
      and projects it\n onto the reachable circle, so its only degeneracy is \"the
      predicted elbow lands ON the arm's own\n axis\" -- 0.036% of the workspace.
      (The projected bend is a tangent field again, so Poincare-Hopf\n still demands
      two such zeros per reach shell; they sit across-body-up and down-back. The
      fade\n band that used to blur them traded a measure-zero core for an antipodal-lerp
      TELEPORT surface in\n healthy workspace -- the \"big swings flip\" bug -- and
      is gone. BasisArmBigSwingFlipTests sweeps\n those exact paths.)\n\n EVERY TEST
      BELOW DRIVES INPUTS THE LIVE RIG CAN ACTUALLY PRODUCE, especially the ones
      the corpus\n never contains. That is the lesson the previous two elbow regressions
      cost, and it is written into\n the tests rather than into a comment nobody
      reads.\n ================================================================================================\n
      </summary>"
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
