  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisElbowProtectRollTests
    MemberName: BasisElbowProtectRollTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Guards BasisElbowProtectCore against spinning the arm about its own
      long axis.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      THE BUG, IN THE USER'S OWN WORDS: \"if i fully extend my arm and then cross
      over my body all the\n way to the left as much as humanly possible the elbow
      does a nasty rotation.\"\n\n Both halves of that sentence are load-bearing,
      and neither alone reproduces it:\n\n   CROSS THE BODY -> the upper arm presses
      into the chest, so the protect ENGAGES. It only ever\n                    
      runs on a torso penetration, which is why nothing else in the workspace shows\n                    
      this.\n   FULLY EXTEND   -> the elbow's circle radius rho collapses to nothing.\n\n
      The protect clears the torso by SWIVELLING the elbow about the shoulder->hand
      axis. That rotation\n buys elbow displacement of rho * theta -- and it costs
      upper-arm ROLL of theta, which does NOT\n scale with rho. So as the arm straightens
      the benefit goes to zero and the cost stays whole: the\n swivel axis IS the
      arm's own long axis by then, and the entire correction lands as spin.\n\n MEASURED
      on the shipped code, along exactly that motion: a steady ~47 degree commanded
      swing, and\n at extension >= 0.999 it becomes 47-51 DEGREES OF UPPER-ARM ROLL
      PER MILLIMETRE OF HAND TRAVEL.\n Disable the protect and the same sweep measures
      0.0. It is unambiguously this file.\n\n \u2B50 WHY NOTHING CAUGHT IT: ROLL
      MOVES NO JOINT. Rotating the arm about shoulder->hand leaves the\n hand exactly
      on target and the elbow (at full extension, on the axis) exactly put. Every
      gate in\n this repo scores POSITIONS -- hand error, elbow travel, reach, clearance,
      mocap accuracy. All of\n them are structurally incapable of seeing a limb spin.
      This is the same blind spot that hid the\n solve cores' 180-degree roll (BasisLimbRollConditioningTests),
      and this is a third instance of it.\n\n \u2B50 AND WHY THE OBVIOUS FIX IS WRONG:
      fading on rho does NOT work. rho = sqrt(upper^2 - (d/2)^2) is\n SQUARE-ROOT
      SINGULAR in the hand position, so its own gradient blows up exactly where the
      fade has\n to be gentle -- it converts the cliff into a steep ramp and measures
      9-10 deg/mm. The fade must key\n on the REACH RATIO, whose derivative is bounded
      by 1/armLen. That is the difference between a fix\n and a relocation, and this
      codebase has relocated a discontinuity and called it a fix before.\n ================================================================================================"
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
