  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisElbowSwingCapRadialTests
    MemberName: BasisElbowSwingCapRadialTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Guards the RADIAL half of BasisElbowSwingCapCore's budget (2026-07-22).\n\n
      ================================================================================================\n
      THE DEFECT THESE PIN.\n\n The cap's budget used to be `MaxGain * angle(prevAxis,
      curAxis)` alone. A hand travelling straight\n ALONG its own arm axis -- every
      punch, push, point-and-retract and straight-line reach -- rotates\n that axis
      by EXACTLY ZERO, so the budget was zero and the bend was FROZEN, not slowed.
      The field\n genuinely moves there (BasisElbowFieldModel.Elbow is affine in
      tipLocal, so at fixed direction the\n perpendicular component is `a + r*b`,
      whose direction rotates with r; BasisSwivelHintCore's\n elbow-down term then
      ramps over reach 0.90->0.99 on top). Measured 22.9-29.8 deg of pole error and\n
      5.3-7.0 cm of solved elbow error against a field model whose own published
      error is 2.07 cm.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      HOW THESE TESTS AVOID BEING TAUTOLOGICAL.\n\n A test that only asserts \"the
      new path is good\" proves nothing -- it would pass just as happily\n against
      a stub. Every claim here is therefore made against a CONTROL that must FAIL:\n\n  
      * TheDefect_IsReal_OnTheRotationOnlyCap is the RULER. It drives the OLD (5-arg)
      overload down\n     the same trajectory and asserts the error is still >= 20
      deg. If someone \"fixes\" the defect by\n     weakening the trajectory, flattening
      the field, or making the harness blind, this test goes\n     green-to-red first
      and the improvement claims below become unclaimable.\n   * TheConditioningGate_IsWhatProtectsTheCore
      asserts BOTH directions at once: gated == old to the\n     bit at a core, AND
      the same code path with the gate DEFEATED (conditioning forced to 1) leaks\n    
      a double-digit flip. The second half is the anti-tautology control -- it proves
      the core check\n     can actually detect a broken gate rather than passing
      because nothing ever moves."
    Remarks: 
