  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisElbowSwivelConditioningTests
    MemberName: BasisElbowSwivelConditioningTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 1
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "The ELBOW's half of the pole singularity, and the proof that the KNEE's
      guard must NOT be copied onto it.\n\n The arm shares BasisSwivelSmootherCore
      with the leg and inherits the same trap: a One-Euro is\n SPEED-ADAPTIVE (`cutoff
      = minCutoff + beta * |velocity|`), so when the arm straightens and the elbow's
      lever\n arm off the shoulder->hand axis collapses, the measured swivel degenerates
      into the direction of a vanishing\n vector -- noise with a huge velocity --
      and the filter reads that as INTENT and opens the cutoff on it.\n\n The elbow's
      beta (0.05) is a quarter of the tracked knee's (0.20), so the effect is milder,
      but it is the\n same defect and it is large: at 90 Hz a 60 deg step drives
      velHat to ~353 deg/s, which lands the cutoff at\n 18.6 Hz and passes 57% of
      the noise through in ONE frame. Conditioning the filter on the lever arm takes\n
      that to ~11%, while a genuinely bent elbow keeps ~48% -- damped where the pole
      is meaningless, responsive\n where it is real.\n\n Two gate groups:\n   1-3.
      The conditioning gates (damped straight / responsive bent / legacy genuinely
      snaps).\n   4.   HalfSpaceGuard_CannotTransferToTheArm -- the one that stops
      someone \"finishing the job\" by pasting the\n        knee's anterior half-space
      onto the elbow. It cannot work, and this proves it without appealing to any\n       
      particular choice of reference direction."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.
    MemberName: Conditioning_CollapsesAsTheArmStraightens
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "An arm reaching FORWARD, `reach` of full extension, elbow swivelled
      `swivelDeg` about the shoulder->hand\n axis away from the body-DOWN reference
      (which is what SmoothElbowSwivel actually uses).\n\n Reaching forward, not
      down, on purpose: the elbow's ReferenceLocal is Vector3.down and its FallbackLocal\n
      is ZERO, so an arm hanging straight down puts the reference COLINEAR with the
      axis and the core correctly\n refuses to smooth at all. A forward reach keeps
      body-down cleanly inside the swivel plane, which is the\n configuration where
      the filter actually runs -- and therefore the one worth gating."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
