  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisFootFrameTests
    MemberName: BasisFootFrameTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Frame gates for the foot-placement sim (BasisFootSimulateJob).\n\n
      Every bug this file exists to catch was the same shape: a rotation read in
      the wrong frame, or read off an\n axis that had gone degenerate. They are grouped
      here because they share a root cause, not a subsystem.\n\n 1. BODY FORWARD
      AT THE POLES. Every bone's facing was obtained by flattening its forward onto
      the ground\n    plane: flat = fwd - up*dot(fwd,up). That is only meaningful
      while the forward HAS horizontal length.\n    Look straight down (or up) and
      the head's forward is parallel to playerUp, the projection collapses to\n   
      ~zero, and normalize() turns float noise into a wildly swinging yaw. The old
      guards let this through --\n    hips and chest required only lengthsq > 0.001,
      i.e. a mere 1.8 degrees off vertical -- while the head's\n    stricter lengthsq
      > 0.1 guard simply DROPPED the head past ~72 degrees of pitch, so the body
      forward\n    lurched as the head crossed the threshold. Neither is a facing;
      both are artifacts.\n\n    The yaw is not lost at the pole, it has only changed
      axes: when a bone's FORWARD goes vertical, its UP\n    axis becomes HORIZONTAL
      and carries the yaw exactly. BoneYaw() reads whichever axis is currently\n   
      horizontal and cross-fades by |flat(fwd)|. These gates sweep pitch through
      the poles INCLUDING exactly\n    +/-90 degrees, because the specific way today's
      cervical bug hid was a threshold branch that the test\n    never crossed. Enumerate
      the branches; cross every threshold.\n\n 2. FOOT ROTATION AT REST. FootRotation()
      builds a frame from the BODY's axes. A humanoid foot bone's local\n    axes
      are NOT the body's -- its +Z commonly runs down the shin or out along the toes,
      entirely\n    rig-dependent -- so returning that frame as the bone's rotation
      is what came out \"toes-up\" and got foot\n    rotation switched off in the
      first place. Re-seating through footAlign (the bone measured IN that frame\n   
      at the T-pose) makes a standing foot reproduce its rest rotation EXACTLY. That
      is an identity, not a\n    tuning: assert it as one, and toes-up can never
      come back.\n\n 3. NaN MUST NOT ESCAPE. A NaN'd transform in Unity PERSISTS
      -- the rig dies and never recovers -- so a\n    single degenerate frame is
      unrecoverable rather than a blip. Worse, the obvious guard shape is wrong:\n   
      `if (bad < threshold) reject` FAILS OPEN on NaN, because NaN < x is false for
      every x. Validity checks\n    must be written as `!(good > threshold)` so NaN
      falls into the reject branch."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
