  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisHumeralTwistElevationCorpusTests
    MemberName: BasisHumeralTwistElevationCorpusTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 1
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: " <summary>\n HOW MUCH HUMERAL AXIAL ARC DO REAL PEOPLE ACTUALLY USE,
      PER ELEVATION BAND?\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. BasisArmSolveCore.HumeralTwistSoftDeg is a FLAT 120 deg
      at every elevation,\n and its own comment justifies that number with a segmented
      corpus audit (\"<=2.5% in every band\")\n THAT IS NOT PINNED BY ANY TEST. The
      number came from an offline sweep that was never landed, so\n there has been
      no baseline to re-run against and no way to referee a proposed change to it.\n\n
      A change IS proposed: make the bound elevation-dependent, carrying the shipped
      120 at 90 deg\n through the clinical glenohumeral arc (~48/71/40 deg total
      at 45/90/135 deg of abduction) by the\n slack factor 120/71 = 1.69, giving
      81 / 120 / 68. That is arithmetically self-consistent. But the\n slack factor
      is fitted to a SINGLE anchor point, and one anchor cannot choose between two
      equally\n available models of what the slack IS:\n\n     MULTIPLICATIVE   whole-complex(e)
      = 1.69 * GH(e)            ->  +-81 @45,  +-68 @135\n     ADDITIVE         whole-complex(e)
      = GH(e) + constant slack  -> +-108 @45, +-104 @135\n\n Both reproduce 120 at
      90 deg exactly. They disagree by 27-37 deg at the ends -- more than the whole\n
      effect being chased. The girdle slack exists because THIS RIG'S CLAVICLE IS
      FROZEN and the humerus\n absorbs the girdle's share; there is no reason that
      share should be proportional to GH axial ROM,\n and clavicular axial rotation
      in fact GROWS with elevation, which is a third shape again.\n\n So this file
      does not pick a model. It measures the quantity both models are trying to predict
      --\n the axial arc real humans occupy at each elevation -- and lets that referee.\n
      ================================================================================================\n\n
      \u2B50 THE MEASUREMENT IS BIND-FREE, ON PURPOSE. The shipped guard bounds twist
      against a T-pose BIND,\n and the corpus has no T-pose; synthesising one would
      make the answer a function of my choice of\n neutral rather than of the corpus.
      So this measures the ARC -- the spread of humeral axial rotation\n within an
      elevation band -- which needs no neutral at all. An arc is exactly what the
      clinical\n figures quote, so it is also the like-for-like comparison.\n\n \u26A0
      THE AXIS IS READ FROM POSITIONS, WHICH IS BOTH HONEST AND SELF-LIMITING. With
      the elbow bent, the\n forearm's offset from the humerus axis IS the humerus's
      axial rotation -- a hinge cannot hide it.\n With the elbow straight that offset
      vanishes and the quantity genuinely does not exist. That is the\n SAME singularity
      BasisElbowAnatomyCore's frame note records the corpus hitting, so the exclusion\n
      below is not a convenience: it is the boundary of what this corpus can referee,
      and it is asserted\n rather than assumed.\n </summary>"
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
