  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisHumeralTwistGuardTests
    MemberName: BasisHumeralTwistGuardTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "NOTHING BOUNDED HUMERAL AXIAL ROTATION. These are the gates on the
      guard that now does.\n\n THE DEFECT, MEASURED IN UNITY: with an elbow tracker
      on an extended arm, rolling the tracker rolled\n the humerus 1:1 THROUGH A
      FULL 360 DEGREES. The clavicle never moved while it happened --\n BasisShoulderSolveCore
      is direction-driven and a pure roll changes no direction, so the girdle\n contributed
      exactly 0.0 deg and never engaged. Every neighbouring degree of freedom was
      bounded\n (elbow height, elbow flexion, forearm roll, wrist relief, clavicle-vs-chest);
      this one was not.\n\n WHAT THE SWEEP BELOW REPRODUCES, and why it is the real
      thing rather than a contrivance: an elbow\n tracker's POSITION is the solver's
      pole, and rolling a forearm strap orbits that position about the\n arm axis.
      The solver obeys the pole -- correctly -- with a swivel about shoulder->hand.
      On a\n near-straight arm the elbow's lever arm has collapsed, so obeying the
      pole is almost pure ROLL of\n the upper arm. Sweeping the pole 0-360 deg therefore
      sweeps the humerus through a full turn, and the\n UNGUARDED control here reaches
      a flat 180 deg of twist.\n\n THE FOUR PROMISES THESE TESTS HOLD THE GUARD TO:\n  
      1. IT BOUNDS, AND THE CONTROL PROVES THE TEST STILL MEASURES. Every sweep runs
      twice -- once with\n      the bind fields fed, once with `default` -- and the
      control must still break 170 deg. A test\n      that only asserted \"small\"
      would pass just as happily if the sweep stopped exciting the DOF.\n   2. IT
      MOVES NOTHING. The correction is applied about shoulder->ELBOW, the humerus's
      own axis, and\n      the elbow LIES on it, so no joint moves at all -- the
      guard writes rotations only. This is the\n      assertion that pins the OPERATOR:
      a regression to a shoulder->hand swivel moves the elbow\n      (measured: 1.6
      cm), and a positional shoulder->elbow swivel moves the HAND. Checking both\n     
      deltas plus the correction's own axis catches every one of those.\n   3. IT
      IS THE EXACT IDENTITY INSIDE THE ENVELOPE. Legal poses take the untouched path
      bit for bit,\n      not \"approximately unchanged\".\n   4. ZERO DECLINES,
      FIELD BY FIELD. Any one of the five bind inputs left at its struct default
      turns\n      the guard off, so every existing caller, test and offline sweep
      stays bit-identical.\n\n AND THE ONE THIS FILE EXISTS FOR MOST: RefAxis_IsBakedPerRig_GuardEngagesOnEveryConvention.\n
      BindHumerusRefAxis is baked per rig by BasisFullBodyIK.BakeHumerusTwistBind
      precisely because a\n HARDCODED axis lands PARALLEL to the bone on rigs whose
      humerus points down its local -Y -- and a\n parallel reference does not fail
      loudly, it projects to zero and the guard DECLINES SILENTLY. That\n test runs
      every rig convention through the real solver, and it also feeds the naive hardcoded
      axis\n to the same solver to show the hazard is real rather than hypothetical.\n\n
      The measurement here mirrors the core's own reference construction (it is the
      definition of the\n quantity being bounded), so it is checked against an INDEPENDENT
      closed form as well: swivelling a\n vector standing alpha off the swivel axis
      by theta twists it by 2*atan(cos(alpha)*tan(theta/2))\n about its own axis.
      The control sweep must match that curve, which is what stops the whole file\n
      being circular."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
