  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisIKEquivarianceTests
    MemberName: BasisIKEquivarianceTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "The cross-space conformance gate for the IK solve cores.\n\n One property
      covers the whole class of coordinate-space bugs:\n\n     EQUIVARIANCE:  Solve(T
      . inputs)  ==  T . Solve(inputs)      for any rigid T = (rotation, translation)\n\n
      Rotate and translate every world-space quantity a core is given -- joints,
      targets, hints, bind axes,\n PlayerUp, bend normals -- and the solved pose
      must come back rotated and translated by exactly the same\n T. Scalars the
      core reports (reach ratio, joint angle, penetration depth) must not move at
      all: they are\n properties of the body, not of where the body happens to be
      standing.\n\n A core that reaches for a WORLD constant -- Vector3.up, Vector3.forward,
      Vector3.down -- instead of the\n body/player frame it was handed cannot satisfy
      this, because the constant does not rotate with T. That is\n exactly how both
      shipped defects looked:\n   - SmoothKneeSwivel measured the knee against world
      forward, so a turn leaked into the knee DOF;\n   - the T-pose capture stored
      a world-axes offset that the bone sim then rotated a second time.\n Neither
      was reachable by the existing per-core gates, which all test one orientation.
      This one is\n orientation-blind by construction, so a leak has nowhere to hide.\n\n
      Being equivariant is also what \"the avatar behaves the same wherever you stand
      and whichever way you\n face\" means, formally: it is the property the whole
      rig is silently assumed to have.\n\n NOT transformed, on purpose: TargetOffset
      and the T-pose binds are LOCAL quantities (a bind rotation\n relative to the
      avatar root), so a global re-orientation of the world must leave them alone.
      Transforming\n them would test a different -- and wrong -- contract."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
