  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisKneeForwardTests
    MemberName: BasisKneeForwardTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Knee-forward (azimuth) sweep for BasisKneeForwardCore and its hand-off
      to the two-bone leg\n solver (BasisLegSolveCore).\n\n Scenario: FOOT trackers,
      no KNEE tracker. The knee pole should aim along the FOOT's toe direction instead
      of\n straight body-forward, so turning a foot turns the knee -- damped by posture:\n  
      - STANDING (leg vertical, toe across the leg): the knee follows the toe by
      ~Coupling. Real humans hold the\n     tibial fore-aft axis ~perpendicular to
      the pelvis in stance (1.65 +/- 5.58 deg; PMC5930825) against a\n     ~5-7 deg
      foot progression angle, so the follow is GENTLE and the knee sits inboard of
      the foot.\n   - RECLINING (leg flattening, toe lining up with the leg): the
      toe stops being a usable azimuth, Upright01\n     fades to 0, and the follow
      with it -- handing the knee's opening off to the butterfly (instep) splay.\n\n
      The pole is fed through the real leg solver exactly as BasisLocalRigDriver
      wires it (HintPosition +\n HintWeight, BendNormal = hips-right), so the knee
      azimuth is measured end-to-end."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisKneeInversionTests
    MemberName: BasisKneeInversionTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 1
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\"I have a foot tracker and a knee hint, I push the foot forward, and
      the knee flips around\" gate.\n\n A knee is a HINGE. It flexes one way -- heel
      toward the backside -- so the knee always bulges ANTERIOR to\n the hip->ankle
      axis. A knee behind that axis is not merely unnatural, it is anatomically unrepresentable.\n
      That makes it a hard constraint, not a tuning knob, and the guard is written
      to make the posterior\n half-space UNREACHABLE rather than merely unlikely.\n\n
      The knee ends up wherever the POLE points, because the solve's SWIVEL step
      rotates the knee ONTO the pole.\n So guarding the pole guards the knee, structurally.
      The two places a pole can be chosen both get the same\n bound, from the same
      shared clamp:\n   - BasisLegSolveCore places the knee on the pole.\n   - BasisSwivelSmootherCore
      can MOVE it afterwards, so it needs the bound too or a lagging filter drags
      the\n     knee back through the joint.\n\n Why the defect exists at all: the
      pole is `hint - hip` projected perpendicular to the hip->ankle axis. As\n the
      leg extends that lever arm collapses, so a few millimetres of tracker offset
      is enough to tip the\n projection across the axis and land the knee posterior
      -- the same pole singularity as the swivel snap\n (BasisKneeSwivelConditioningTests),
      seen from the other side. The BEND step cannot save it either: fed an\n over-reaching
      target it SATURATES at a straight leg rather than refusing, so the only place
      inversion can be\n stopped is at the pole.\n\n The gates, and the third is
      what stops this file rotting into a tautology:\n   1. The knee is NEVER posterior
      -- hint swept a full 360deg, at every reach including the singular ones.\n  
      2. Legitimate poses are UNTOUCHED (butterfly splay at its 60deg limit passes
      through bit-for-bit).\n   3. The sweep actually REACHES the inverting region,
      and the guard is what stops it -- proven by showing\n      the raw pole demands
      a posterior knee on those samples, and that the legacy (unguarded) smoother\n     
      really does invert. Without this, gates 1-2 could pass for entirely unrelated
      reasons."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
