  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisKneeSwivelConditioningTests
    MemberName: BasisKneeSwivelConditioningTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 1
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\"The leg is very snappy when I move the lower-leg hint\" gate.\n\n
      A two-bone knee's bend PLANE is set by the pole, but a STRAIGHT leg has no
      bend plane: the knee sits on\n the hip->foot axis and the swivel angle about
      that axis is undefined. `footHeightOffset` is deliberately\n clamped so \"the
      legs fully extend when standing\", which parks a standing leg at hip->foot
      ~= thigh+shin --\n i.e. permanently ON that singularity. There the raw swivel
      is noise thrashing through large angles.\n\n The trap: BasisSwivelFilterCore
      is a One-Euro, and a One-Euro is SPEED-ADAPTIVE --\n `cutoff = minCutoff +
      beta * |velocity|`. Its premise is \"a fast signal is a signal the user MEANT,
      so\n stop filtering it\". At a singularity that premise inverts: the noise
      has a huge velocity, the filter reads\n it as intent, opens the cutoff wide,
      and passes the garbage through unfiltered. A speed-adaptive filter is\n ACTIVELY
      HARMFUL at a singularity -- it amplifies exactly what it was added to damp.\n\n
      The fix conditions the filter's responsiveness on the pole's lever arm\n (BasisSwivelSmootherResult.Conditioning
      = sin of the angle between the upper bone and the\n hip->foot axis; 0 = singular,
      1 = fully folded), so beta -> 0 as the leg straightens and the cutoff stops\n
      opening on noise, while a genuinely bent knee keeps full responsiveness.\n\n
      Three gates, and the third is the one that stops this file rotting into a tautology:\n  
      1. STRAIGHT is damped.\n   2. BENT is responsive (so the fix did not simply
      glue the knee in place -- deliberate shin motion still tracks).\n   3. LEGACY
      (ConditionOnPole off) is SNAPPY while straight -- proving the guard measures
      the real defect\n      and that a revert fails loudly, rather than the first
      two gates passing for some unrelated reason."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.
    MemberName: Conditioning_IsZeroAtFullExtension_AndOneWhenFolded
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Builds a leg whose hip->foot distance is `reach` of full extension,
      with the knee swivelled\n `swivelDeg` about the hip->foot axis away from the
      body-forward reference.\n\n Frame: hip at origin, foot straight below it, so
      the axis is world-down and the reference direction\n (body forward, identity
      body rotation) is world-forward and lies cleanly in the swivel plane.\n For
      thigh == shin this gives conditioning == sqrt(1 - reach^2) analytically, which
      is what makes the\n reach values below map onto known conditioning numbers."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
