  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisKneeSwivelWrapTests
    MemberName: BasisKneeSwivelWrapTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 1
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\"The knees have bad clicking rotationally\" gate -- the two +-180
      DISCONTINUITIES in the knee swivel path.\n\n A click is a DERIVATIVE defect:
      a large output jump for a small input step. Every other knee gate in this\n
      repo scores a POSE (never posterior, foot on target, lever arm bounded, gate
      open/closed), and a pose-shaped\n assert is structurally incapable of seeing
      a jump -- the broken code satisfies all of them at every sample\n either side
      of the cut. So every test here measures a STEP, and each one is paired with
      an anti-tautology\n twin that proves the old formulation fails it.\n\n DEFECT
      1 -- the anterior guard folded at the posterior ray. BasisLegSolveCore.ClampKneeSwivelDeg\n
      was monotone in |angle|, so clamp(+179.9) = +89.30 while clamp(-179.9) = -89.30.
      Those two inputs are the\n SAME knee direction give or take a fifth of a degree,
      so crossing the back of the leg flipped the knee 178.6\n deg. Measured by finite
      difference on the shipped cores: 714x gain in the solve (at EVERY reach ratio
      from\n 0.70 to 1.00 -- so it is NOT the extension singularity) and 698x through
      the smoother. The jump also clears\n BasisSwivelSmootherCore's 25 deg reseed
      threshold, so the One-Euro is thrown away rather than damping it.\n\n DEFECT
      2 -- the smoother's reference REVERSED. refDir = ProjectOnPlane(body-forward,
      hip->ankle) does not\n merely shrink as the leg swings toward body-forward,
      it passes through zero and comes out backwards. The leg\n reaches that direction
      whenever it is straight out in front: sitting on the floor, a front kick, supine
      with\n the legs up. Fixed by TRANSPORTING the reference from body-down instead
      of projecting it.\n\n DEFECT 3 -- the near-colinear and HintDistrust eases
      are Vector3.Slerp toward bendPole, and a slerp between\n ANTI-PARALLEL directions
      has no shortest great circle, so the arc flips to the far side of the sphere
      as the\n pole crosses -bendPole. Measured 610x knee gain, and reachable: a
      shin tracker on a standing leg sits at\n poleSin ~0.34, inside the band where
      the ease runs. Not fixable by changing the interpolation -- you cannot\n continuously
      fade a direction toward a fixed direction (the identity map on a circle has
      degree 1 and the\n constant map 0, and no homotopy connects them). Fixed by
      applying the anterior guard BEFORE the eases, which\n bounds the pole strictly
      inside the anterior half-space and makes the anti-parallel input unreachable.\n\n
      \u26A0\uFE0F THE TWO FIXES ARE NOT SEPARABLE, which is why they land together
      and are gated together. Measured worst\n knee jump for a 0.5 deg step of leg
      pitch through body-forward, all four combinations:\n\n        clamp   reference     
      worst knee jump\n        OLD     projected            90.70 deg     <- shipping
      before\n        NEW     projected           179.00 deg     <- clamp fix ALONE
      is a REGRESSION\n        OLD     transported           0.50 deg\n        NEW    
      transported           0.50 deg     <- both\n\n Closing the circle sends a dead-posterior
      pole to dead-anterior, which is only safe once the reference it is\n measured
      against has stopped flipping underneath it."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
