  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisLegHintReachTests
    MemberName: BasisLegHintReachTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Reach-preservation gate for the knee hint (BasisLegSolveCore).\n\n
      \u26A0\uFE0F THESE GATES ARE RED. They reproduce a real, unfixed defect; they
      are not a regression.\n\n The foot is a COMMANDED target: whatever the knee
      hint does to the pole, the foot must stay exactly where\n it was told to be.
      It does not. Worst measured: 145 mm on a synthetic sweep (hint 180 deg around
      the leg\n axis), and 27 mm on real human motion (CMU 02_01) -- a visible slide
      on a foot tracker, and ~25x the\n \"0.13% of leg length\" previously believed
      to be the worst case.\n\n ROOT CAUSE -- it is the BEND step, not the hint step.
      `deltaR` rotates the shin about the knee by an angle\n that TriangleAngle picks
      so the hip->ankle distance comes out equal to the target distance. That derivation\n
      is only valid if the rotation axis is PERPENDICULAR to the hip-knee-ankle plane.
      The leg's axis is\n Cross(hint, bc), and it is then slerped toward BendNormal
      by the pole-colinear guard and again by the\n near-full-extension guard. A
      blended axis is not perpendicular to that plane, so the interior angle at the\n
      knee does not land where the maths assumed, |ac| != the target distance, and
      the follow-up rootDelta can\n only correct the DIRECTION to the target, never
      the LENGTH. The arm papers over exactly this with a\n 12-iteration bisection
      that backs the hint off until the hand is on target again; the leg has no such
      pass.\n\n THE FIX (not attempted here -- BasisLegSolveCore has a revert history
      and this is a real rewrite, not a\n one-liner): place the knee analytically.
      Use the pole to fix the bend PLANE, then solve the knee's position\n in that
      plane by trigonometry from |ab|, |bc| and the target distance. That hits the
      target exactly, in\n every configuration, with no bisection.\n\n The sweep
      visits the hint all the way around the leg axis so the anti-parallel case is
      actually exercised,\n rather than assuming it never comes up. It does: it is
      the worst case."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
