  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisMocapMotionQualityTests
    MemberName: BasisMocapMotionQualityTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 1
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\"Is it human? Is it natural?\" -- measured, not judged.\n\n The accuracy
      suite next door asks whether the solved elbow is in the RIGHT PLACE. This one
      asks\n whether it got there the way a human would. The two are genuinely independent:
      a solved elbow can\n sit 2 cm from the truth on every single frame and still
      buzz at 30 Hz, sit on a plateau, or arrive\n 150 ms late. Mean error cannot
      see any of that, because each frame, taken alone, looks fine. The\n badness
      only exists BETWEEN frames -- and until this file there was nothing in the
      project that\n looked between frames.\n\n Because the corpus gives us a real
      human's elbow doing the same motion, every number here is a\n DIFFERENTIAL
      against ground truth. That removes the argument: the question is never \"is
      1200 units\n of jerk a lot\", it is \"did our elbow move like THAT elbow did,
      given the same hand to follow\".\n\n Thresholds are PROVISIONAL and loose --
      see BasisMocapMotionQuality. They catch \"that is not a\n human arm\" and nothing
      subtler. The table this prints is what tightens them, exactly as the\n accuracy
      layer landed (\"there is no honest threshold until the number exists\"). Read
      the table,\n then ratchet."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
