  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisPostureCorpusTests
    MemberName: BasisPostureCorpusTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 1
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "THE POSTURE CORPUS: what a real pelvis does, across every way a human
      gets their head low.\n\n The 20-clip corpus next door is calibrated for the
      ARM and the KNEE and its numbers are quoted all over\n this project, so it
      is left BYTE-IDENTICAL. These clips live in a subfolder that the other tests'\n
      non-recursive GetFiles cannot see, and they were chosen from the CMU index
      BY DESCRIPTION to span the\n one axis that matters:\n\n   SQUAT / SIT     
      pelvis rides the head down (13_29 squats, 75_17..75_20 sits graded by seat
      height,\n                    64_26..64_30 picking a ball off the floor, 139_16
      getting up off the ground)\n   WAIST-BEND       pelvis stays high, spine folds
      (26_10/11 bend+lift, 02_06 bend over and scoop up,\n                    13_23/14_16
      sweeping a floor, 77_06/07/08 poking about on the ground)\n   UPRIGHT         
      walks and stands -- and these are the most important clips in the folder. Without\n                   
      them the fit has never seen \"doing nothing\" and would cheerfully drop the
      pelvis of\n                    a user who is just standing there. The model
      is fitted THROUGH THE ORIGIN for the\n                    same reason: at zero
      head drop and zero lean it must return exactly zero, as an\n                   
      algebraic identity rather than as a hope.\n\n This file DUMPS the features
      and REPORTS the coverage. The fit runs on this dump and nothing else --\n the
      same one-pipeline rule the swivel models are under, and for the same reason:
      a model fitted in one\n frame and evaluated in another does not degrade, it
      produces confident garbage. It has already cost\n this project one full cycle
      (3.77% in Python, 31% in the harness, a mirror the two disagreed about)."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisProportionMismatchTests
    MemberName: BasisProportionMismatchTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\u2B50 THE AVATAR IS NOT THE USER. Accuracy when the body driving the
      solver is not the body that\n produced the targets.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      THE GAP THIS CLOSES\n\n Every accuracy number this project quotes -- BasisMocapAccuracyTests,
      BasisDynamicCorpusTests,\n BasisSpineCorpusAccuracyTests -- was measured with
      a CMU subject driving THEIR OWN proportions. The\n hand target and the arm
      it hangs off came from the same skeleton, so |shoulder->hand| / armLen <= 1\n
      on 100% of frames, by the triangle inequality, always. A real VR user is NEVER
      in that situation:\n the controller reports where the USER's hand is, and the
      avatar's arm is whatever length the avatar\n happens to have.\n\n That is not
      a small perturbation on top of the measured error. It is a DIFFERENT INPUT
      DISTRIBUTION,\n and it has already shipped a bug. From the project's own post-mortem,
      verbatim:\n\n     \"THE HARNESS'S INPUTS WERE NOT THE RUNTIME'S INPUTS... The
      mocap corpus only ever contains a\n      hand ON the arm, so |tipLocal| <=
      1 in 100% of training data. The live rig is handed the RAW\n      CONTROLLER
      TARGET, which sails past the avatar's reach constantly -- anyone whose real
      arms are\n      longer than their avatar's is outside the fit box permanently.
      A cubic with coefficients up to\n      15 out there is not 'approximate', it
      is a random number generator.\"\n\n The fix was a hard domain clamp -- saturate,
      never extrapolate (BasisElbowFieldModel.Elbow,\n BasisArmElbowNeuralFieldModel.Elbow,
      BasisLegSwivelModel.SwivelRad all open with\n `t = len > 1 ? tipLocal / len
      : tipLocal`). BasisSwivelHintConformanceTests pins that clamp on a\n SYNTHETIC
      T-posed rig with random directions. Nothing pinned it against realistic mismatch
      on real\n motion, and nothing anywhere measured HOW OFTEN a mismatched user
      actually leaves the box, or what\n the accuracy costs as the mismatch grows.
      Those three things are this file.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      HOW THIS DIFFERS FROM BasisBodyProportionTests -- THEY ARE COMPLEMENTARY, NOT
      OVERLAPPING\n\n   BasisBodyProportionTests asks: does the solver behave the
      SAME on a differently-proportioned body?\n   It is an INVARIANCE suite. Its
      headline, Solve_IsScaleInvariant_FromChibiToGiant, scales the body\n   AND
      the pose together and demands identical angles -- uniform scale must be a NO-OP.
      Its bodies\n   are synthetic profiles built from height fractions, its poses
      are synthetic landmarks, and in\n   every single case the target belongs to
      the SAME body as the limb solving for it. It never\n   produces the mismatch
      condition at all.\n\n   This file asks the opposite question: what happens
      when the scale is NOT shared? The avatar is one\n   size, the effector targets
      come from a human of another size, and there is no invariance to appeal\n  
      to -- the reach ratio genuinely changes, frames genuinely leave the fitted
      domain, and the error\n   genuinely grows. Uniform scale is a no-op; MISMATCH
      is not, and that is the whole point.\n\n   The one place they touch is Arm_BeyondReachInputs_FallShortCleanly_AcrossBodyProportions,
      which\n   holds the same shortfall property this file re-checks -- on synthetic
      landmark poses that happen\n   to overshoot. This file drives it from 13,785
      frames of real human motion instead, and it is\n   reused here (same 0.005
      limb-length bound) rather than re-derived.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      GUARDING THE RULER\n\n At 1.0x the rescaled clip must be BIT-IDENTICAL to the
      original and must reproduce every existing\n accuracy number exactly. If it
      does not, the rescale is corrupting the motion and every mismatch\n number
      below is fiction. That guard is mandatory here because this project has shipped
      several\n metrics that were quietly lying -- a pop detector whose count was
      identical whether its supposed\n cause was present or absent, and a smoothness
      metric that scored over-smoothed mush BETTER than a\n real human. So: an identity
      guard that can fail (BasisClipProportion does not short-circuit at 1.0),\n
      a structural guard the identity guard provably cannot catch (a wrong parent/group
      table), an\n anatomical guard (BasisBvhLoader.Validate on every rescaled clip),
      and a POSITIVE CONTROL on the\n out-of-domain counter itself -- it must be
      non-zero where the cause is present and it must move\n monotonically with the
      cause.\n\n \u26A0 UNMEASURED. At the time of writing this file has never been
      run: the Unity suite is executed\n centrally and the numbers below do not exist
      yet. Every bound asserted here is therefore either\n exact (bit equality, counts,
      monotonicity), reused from a test that WAS calibrated\n (BasisBodyProportionTests'
      0.005 limb-lengths), or explicitly labelled a DIVERGENCE DETECTOR -- a\n bound
      with orders of magnitude of daylight on both sides of it, chosen because there
      is nothing in\n between. The sweep table is report-only. The house doctrine
      is no honest threshold until the number\n exists, and inventing measured-sounding
      headroom would be worse than saying it is not measured.\n\n Corpus: the ROOT
      tier only, via a NON-RECURSIVE Directory.GetFiles. posture/ (44), dynamic/
      (29) and\n slow/ (16) are deliberately isolated so adding clips to one tier
      cannot re-base another tier's\n numbers, and this file's numbers are quoted
      against the same 20 clips the accuracy baseline uses."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.
    MemberName: TheJointHierarchy_ListsEveryParentBeforeItsChild
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 1
    ParamNames:
