  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.
    MemberName: TheDomainClamp_Saturates_OnRealHumanTargetsBeyondAShortAvatarsReach
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 5
    ParamNames:
    - 0
    - 0
    - 0
    - 0f
    - List<string>(
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\u2B50\u2B50 THE SHIPPED BUG'S GUARD, DRIVEN BY REAL MISMATCH.\n\n
      BasisSwivelHintConformanceTests.TheModel_RefusesToExtrapolate already pins
      saturation -- on a\n static synthetic T-posed rig, with random directions,
      ARM ONLY. This drives the same clamp from\n 13,785 frames of real human motion
      against a real short avatar (arm 0.80x, leg 0.85x), through\n the real body
      frames BasisSwivelHintCore.BuildFrame produces, and it covers the LEG model
      too --\n BasisLegSwivelModel is the 3rd-order polynomial with coefficients
      up to 15 that the post-mortem\n is actually about, and its saturation was not
      pinned anywhere.\n\n THE ASSERTION IS EXACT, AND IT CAN BE. Multiplying a float
      by 2 or 4 is exact in binary floating\n point, and scaling by a power of two
      commutes with IEEE rounding through every operation the\n clamp performs: dot,
      sqrt and divide. So for any tipLocal already outside the domain,\n     t =
      tipLocal / |tipLocal|\n is BIT-IDENTICAL whether the model is handed tipLocal,
      2*tipLocal or 4*tipLocal -- and therefore\n so is everything downstream of
      it. No tolerance is needed and none is invented.\n\n GUARDS: the `len > 1f
      ? tipLocal / len : tipLocal` clamp at the head of BasisElbowFieldModel.Elbow,\n
      BasisArmElbowNeuralFieldModel.Elbow and BasisLegSwivelModel.SwivelRad. Delete
      any of them and this\n fails on the first out-of-domain frame. (A failure could
      also mean the toolchain began fusing\n multiply-add, which would break the
      exactness argument rather than the clamp -- also worth knowing.)\n\n THE POSITIVE
      CONTROL IS THE OTHER HALF OF THIS TEST. A saturation check that never sees
      an\n out-of-domain frame passes vacuously and proves nothing, which is precisely
      how this project's\n pop detector managed to report the same count with and
      without its cause. So the beyond-reach\n sample count is asserted non-zero,
      and it is reported."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
