    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.
    MemberName: TheHint_StopsRespondingToOvershoot_WhenTheAvatarsLimbShrinksPastReach
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 1
    ParamNames:
    - List<string>(
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "The same saturation property END TO END, through the shipped entry
      point\n (BasisSwivelHintCore.ArmHint / LegHint) rather than the bare model,
      on the input the runtime\n actually varies: THE AVATAR'S LIMB LENGTH. Halving
      the avatar's arm against a fixed human hand\n target is literally \"the same
      user on a smaller avatar\", and past full reach the elbow direction\n must
      stop responding to it.\n\n GUARDS: that nothing between the model and the hint
      (the tuck, the elbow-down blend at extension,\n the world map) re-introduces
      a dependence on how far past reach the target is.\n HEADROOM: NOT MEASURED
      -- this test has never been run. 0.5 deg is a DIVERGENCE DETECTOR, not a\n
      ratchet: with the clamp intact the two hints differ only by the rounding of
      one world-space\n add/subtract at metre magnitudes (order 1e-5 deg), and without
      it the affine field swings the\n elbow by tens of degrees. There is nothing
      in between, so a loose bound is the honest one. Tighten\n it to the measured
      value once the number exists."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
