  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisShoulderInvariantNetTests
    MemberName: BasisShoulderInvariantNetTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "THE SAME INVARIANT NET, OVER THE SHOULDER GIRDLE PRE-SOLVE.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      BasisShoulderSolveCore is where two of the seven repeating classes have already
      bitten:\n\n   A WRONG FRAME. Everything downstream reads the girdle frame as
      X=lateral, Y=up, Z=forward -- the\n   raise/depression split, the shrug's hang
      gate, the retraction's posterior dot, the\n   elevation-vs-protraction axis
      split. Those are ANATOMY, and the chest BONE only carries them on a\n   rig
      that happens to be bound axis-aligned. On an ordinary Blender export (bone-Y-up,
      an X-90 bind)\n   a plain hanging arm reads as bone-local -Z -- dead ahead
      of the shoulder -- so the hang gate shuts,\n   the posterior dot saturates,
      and an IDLE arm takes a full scapular retraction and saturates the\n   girdle
      clamp. That ships as \"this avatar's shoulders are permanently hunched\", which
      reads as a\n   broken avatar rather than as an IK bug, and the genuine posterior
      reach loses its retraction.\n   ChestBind exists to cancel exactly that, and
      it DECLINES when zero -- so the invariant and its own\n   control are both
      already in the core.\n\n   A GUARD THAT CANNOT FIRE / A GATE THAT FAILS THE
      WRONG WAY. `elbowTrust` fails OPEN on purpose:\n   TposeElbowLength == 0 means
      \"the caller did not bake one\", not \"the tracker is wrong\", and reading\n  
      absence as maximum doubt would collapse the whole solve to the hand-path reach
      gate for every\n   caller that omits it. Which way a gate fails on missing
      data is exactly the kind of thing that is\n   obvious once stated and invisible
      otherwise, so it is stated here as a test.\n\n Plus the classes it has NOT
      yet been bitten by, asserted before it is: continuity through every gate\n
      (the girdle has five -- setting, reach, hang, rise, posterior -- and a swing
      that can reach 180 deg,\n where FromToRotation's axis becomes arbitrary), boundedness,
      twist-freedom measured INDEPENDENTLY of\n the core's own bookkeeping, determinism,
      and degenerate-input hygiene.\n ================================================================================================"
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
