  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisShoulderParentFrameTests
    MemberName: BasisShoulderParentFrameTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "THE CLAVICLE'S PARENT IS THE UPPERCHEST, AND THE GIRDLE FRAME HAS TO
      BE THE PARENT'S.\n\n THE DEFECT: BasisFullBodyIK.SolveShoulder read HandleChest
      for the girdle frame. The clavicle's\n actual parent is the UpperChest, and
      the result is written with SetRotation -- which is\n SetWorldRotation, so the
      parent chain is DISCARDED and whatever frame the solve was built in is the\n
      frame the bone lands in. SolveSpine writes the UpperChest TWICE beforehand,
      independently of the\n Chest (DistributeSpineBend's pitch/roll/routed-twist,
      and ApplyArmSwingChestFollow), so at runtime\n the UpperChest is genuinely
      somewhere the Chest does not know about: up to 0.75x the torso twist,\n about
      30 deg on a 40 deg rotation. Every one of those degrees landed on the clavicle
      as error.\n Both the live read and the TposeChestRot bake now take Mapping.Upperchest,
      with a Chest fallback.\n\n WHAT IS GATED HERE, AND HOW. BasisShoulderSolveCore
      takes the girdle frame as two inputs -- ChestRot\n (live) and TposeChestRot
      (bind) -- so at this level the fix is \"which frame gets passed in\", and the\n
      core cannot be asked which bone that was. What CAN be pinned, exactly and without
      the job, is the\n consequence, and it is the whole reason the frame matters:\n\n    
      THE GIRDLE ROTATION THE CORE REPORTS IS THE ROTATION THE BONE ACTUALLY RECEIVES
      RELATIVE TO ITS\n     PARENT -- IF AND ONLY IF THE FRAME IT WAS GIVEN IS THE
      PARENT'S.\n\n The core's own contract is anatomical: AppliedAngleDeg is a girdle
      swing away from the authored\n bind, twist-stripped and clamped to MaxShoulderDeg.
      Measure the result the way the rig will actually\n see it -- inverse(live UpperChest)
      * ShoulderRotation, against inverse(bind UpperChest) *\n TposeShoulderRot --
      and fed the UpperChest that measurement equals AppliedAngleDeg to floating\n
      point. Fed the Chest it does not: it is off by the UpperChest's independent
      motion, the clamp stops\n bounding anything, and the authored bind clavicle
      is not recovered even when the arm is at rest.\n\n NOT GATED HERE (it needs
      an Animator, a PlayableGraph and the constraint job, none of which are\n reachable
      from an EditMode unit test): that BasisFullBodyIK.SolveShoulder reads HandleUpperChest
      and\n that Create() bakes TposeChestRot from Mapping.Upperchest. These tests
      gate the CONSEQUENCE of that\n wiring, not the wiring itself. Nor is \"unchanged
      when UpperChest is Chest\" a discovery at this level\n -- the frame arrives
      as one input, so it is definitional; it is asserted below as a bit-identity\n
      regression on the fallback path rather than dressed up as more than it is."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
