  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisSpineAnatomyCorpusTests
    MemberName: BasisSpineAnatomyCorpusTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 1
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "IS THE ENVELOPE RIGHT? \u2014 the anatomical ROM table, measured against
      35,081 frames of real humans.\n\n The unit tests next door (BasisSpineAnatomyTests)
      prove the guard's MATHS: that it cannot be escaped,\n that it is the identity
      on legal poses, that it does not pop. They cannot tell you whether the NUMBERS\n
      in the table are the right numbers. That is what the corpus is for, and it
      is a different kind of\n question -- not \"is the code correct\" but \"is the
      anatomy correct\".\n\n THE RULE THAT KEEPS THIS HONEST, AND IT RUNS THE OPPOSITE
      WAY TO EVERY OTHER FIT IN THIS PROJECT:\n the limits are NOT fitted to the
      corpus. They are max-voluntary-ROM from the clinical literature --\n what a
      spine CAN do -- and the corpus gets a VETO ONLY. Motion capture records what
      people DID, and\n CMU subjects walk around a lab; they do not side-bend to
      end range. Fit the limit to that and you\n encode \"didn't\" as \"can't\",
      then clip the first VR user who leans over to look at something.\n\n So the
      acceptance test is: the guard must fire on almost NONE of real human motion,
      and everything it\n DOES touch must be past the 99th percentile -- the anatomically-impossible
      tail that CMU's 3-joint\n spine produces as retargeting slop (a 35 deg lumbar
      twist; a 100 deg lumbar flexion). If the guard\n were firing in the fat of
      the distribution, the table would be wrong and this would fail.\n\n It exercises
      the SHIPPING functions -- BasisSpineAnatomy.BuildRestFrame, .Decompose, .Clamp
      -- on real\n poses, so it is also a live regression guard on the exact code
      the rig runs, not a re-implementation."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisSpineAnatomyTests
    MemberName: BasisSpineAnatomyTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 1
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "THE RULER, NOT THE THING BEING MEASURED. These pin BasisSpineAnatomyCore's
      maths: the swing-twist\n decomposition, the elliptical cone, the saturation,
      the identity, the equivariance. The corpus tests\n next door (BasisSpineAnatomyCorpusTests)
      ask the other question -- whether the ENVELOPE is right --\n against 35,081
      frames of real humans.\n\n A guard is only worth having if it CANNOT be escaped
      and DOES NOT fire on legal poses. Both of those\n are properties, not tolerances,
      and both are tested here by exhaustion rather than by example."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
