    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisSpineBendOverGroundTruthTests
    MemberName: BasisSpineBendOverGroundTruthTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 1
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "WHAT A REAL HUMAN'S PELVIS DOES WHEN THEY BEND OVER -- measured, so
      the spine can stop being argued about.\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      THE TWO COMPLAINTS, FROM IN-HEADSET TESTING:\n   (A) \"looking down forces
      chest to ROTATE, not POSITION -- we need a more stable looking down\"\n   (B)
      \"gamer neck: when bending over the body does not pull itself DOWN enough,
      so the neck looks\n        like a tortoise\"\n\n Both are the same defect wearing
      two hats: THE TORSO ROTATES WHERE IT SHOULD TRANSLATE. And it has\n to, because
      in this rig the chest bone's position is not an IK target at all -- it is pure
      FK, hanging\n off the hips. The entire translate budget in the spine subsystem
      is a 1.5 cm hips / 2.5 cm chest nudge\n inside BasisCervicalSolveCore, and
      it only unlocks past 50 degrees of gaze. Everything else the torso\n can do
      about a head that has moved is turn.\n\n THE HEAD IS A HARD CONSTRAINT -- it
      is welded to the HMD. So every centimetre the body declines to\n travel is
      a centimetre the NECK has to find. That is the tortoise, and it is arithmetic,
      not taste.\n ================================================================================================\n\n
      This file does not assert taste. It reads the CMU bend-over clips -- 26_09
      (bend over / pick up),\n 143_11 (bend + pick up a box), 69_70 (squat / pick
      up), 143_18 (sit down / get up) -- and measures the\n couplings the solver
      is supposed to reproduce:\n\n   VERTICAL     how far does the pelvis drop per
      metre the head drops?\n   HORIZONTAL   as the head travels FORWARD of the feet,
      which way does the pelvis go?\n\n The second one matters most, because the
      shipped answer is a CONSTANT, it is applied with NO regard to\n what the body
      is doing, and I want to know its sign from a human rather than from an argument:\n\n    
      BasisVirtualSpineCore.ComputeRealisticHipsXZBurst\n         hipsXZ = lerp(feetMidXZ,
      headXZ, FootPendulumLeanFrac = 0.20)   // feet tracked\n         hipsXZ = lerp(headBaselineXZ,
      headXZ, CounterbalanceFollowFrac = 0.25)\n\n Both lerp the pelvis TOWARD the
      head, and I fully expected the corpus to call that a sign error -- a\n bending
      human's pelvis slides BACK over the heels, which is what \"counterbalance\"
      means.\n\n \u2B50 THE CORPUS REFUSED TO SAY THAT, AND WHAT IT SAID INSTEAD
      IS MORE USEFUL.\n\n The vertical coupling a real human uses is NOT A CONSTANT.
      It is 0.23 for a waist-bend over a box\n (143_11: the pelvis stays high and
      the spine folds) and 0.88 for a squat (69_70: the pelvis rides the\n head almost
      1:1). Same head drop, completely different pelvis. The rig ships ONE saturating
      law for\n both, so it cannot help being wrong twice -- and it is: on the squat
      it holds the pelvis 32.8 cm ABOVE\n where a real one goes, and on the waist-bend
      it slightly over-drops.\n\n The horizontal slopes tell the same story from
      the other side (-0.25, -0.51, -0.72, then +0.99 on the\n sit), which is why
      the sibling test does NOT assert a sign on them: pooling a squat and a waist-bend\n
      into one slope produces a number with no referent. The thing that separates
      the two motions is HOW FAR\n FORWARD THE HEAD HAS TRAVELLED -- and that is
      exactly the input the rig's vertical law never looks at.\n\n So the finding
      is not \"flip a sign\". It is: THE PELVIS NEEDS AN INPUT IT IS NOT BEING GIVEN,
      and until\n it has one, no single value of these constants can be right. The
      32.8 cm is the size of the prize.\n\n REPORT-ONLY on the measurement, ASSERTING
      on the properties. Read the log; the numbers are the spec."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
