  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisSpineGazeContaminationTests
    MemberName: BasisSpineGazeContaminationTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 1
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\"LOOKING DOWN FORCES CHEST TO ROTATE\" -- is the rig reading GAZE
      as a LEAN?\n\n ================================================================================================\n
      THE SUSPECT, in one line. BasisSpineBendCore.Solve:\n\n     bendPitchDeg =
      atan2(headDir.z, headDir.y) - atan2(chestDir.z, chestDir.y)     // headDir
      = hips->HEAD\n\n The spine's forward bend is the angle between \"hips to chest\"
      and \"hips to HEAD\". But the head is not\n on the spine -- it sits on the
      END of the neck, and it ORBITS that neck when you nod. So a user who\n gazes
      down without moving their torso an inch still swings the head TARGET forward
      and down, the\n hips->head vector tips over, and the solver reads a forward
      lean that never happened. It pitches the\n chest to match.\n\n That is the
      report, verbatim: \"looking down forces chest to rotate not position issue
      we need a more\n stable looking down\".\n ================================================================================================\n\n
      This file does not argue about that. It measures it TWICE, from two independent
      directions, because a\n mechanism I can only demonstrate one way is a mechanism
      I have probably talked myself into:\n\n   1. GEOMETRY -- feed the shipping
      core a head that has ONLY rotated about the neck, with the torso\n      byte-identical,
      and read out how much chest bend it invents. No corpus, no fitting, no opinion.\n\n  
      2. REAL HUMANS -- and this is the part that says what the answer SHOULD be.
      A human looking down\n      does flex their upper spine a little; the question
      was never \"zero\", it is \"how much\", and only\n      the corpus can say.
      If the rig's invented bend is within a couple of degrees of what a human\n     
      actually does, there is no bug here and I should go and look somewhere else."
    Remarks: 
