  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisSpineYawPoleTests
    MemberName: BasisSpineYawPoleTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "\"Look down at my chest, turn my head left and right across the middle,
      and the body SNAPS\"\n regression tests for the torso heading -- BasisVirtualSpineCore.ExtractYawBurst.\n\n
      That function is the single source of the torso's facing direction: its output
      becomes\n torsoYawTarget, which aims the hips, the hips' forward bias, and
      the chest/spine yaw targets.\n It used to flatten the head's FORWARD axis into
      the horizontal plane and take its azimuth --\n but forward carries NO azimuth
      once the gaze is vertical, so the azimuth's gain is 1/cos(gazePitch):\n 5.8x
      at 80 degrees of look-down and unbounded at 90, where it flips a full 180 degrees
      as the gaze\n crosses the pole. Chin tucked to the chest, a 10-degree head
      turn threw the torso 183 degrees.\n The 1e-12 guard was orders of magnitude
      too small to fire; the damage lands long before the\n projection is literally
      zero.\n\n Taking the yaw as the TWIST of a swing-twist decomposition about
      world up has no projection to\n collapse. Its gain is 1/cos(gazePitch / 2)
      -- bounded by sqrt(2) over every gaze a head can reach --\n so the singularity
      moves from 90 degrees (which you hit looking at your own chest) out to 180\n
      (upside down and facing backwards, which you cannot).\n\n Sign convention matches
      Unity and the live job: a positive rotation about X is look-DOWN,\n a positive
      rotation about Y is turn-RIGHT."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
