  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisSwivelFrameInvarianceTests
    MemberName: BasisSwivelFrameInvarianceTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Reference-frame gate for BasisSwivelSmootherCore (the shared body of\n
      BasisFullIKConstraintJob.SmoothElbowSwivel and SmoothKneeSwivel).\n\n A swivel
      angle is only meaningful relative to a reference direction. Both smoothers
      used a WORLD\n constant for that reference -- the elbow world-down, the knee
      world-forward -- while the pole they\n measure is a body vector that co-rotates
      with the player. So a rigid body rotation showed up as a\n change in the measured
      swivel, the One-Euro (1.0-1.5 Hz floor) lagged that phantom change, and the\n
      smoother then actively swung the limb toward a stale, world-locked pole. The
      knee's comment claims\n \"damps swivel jitter without lagging a real turn\";
      with a world reference that claim is false.\n\n The pre-existing BasisLegTwistSmoothingTests
      could not see this: it feeds the FILTER a synthetic\n swivel signal and proves
      the filter tracks it. The defect is upstream of the filter -- a turn should\n
      not produce a swivel signal at all. These gates therefore assert on the MEASUREMENT,
      not the filter.\n\n The invariant, stated once: for a limb held rigid in the
      body frame, the measured swivel must be\n unchanged under ANY rigid motion
      of the body (translation, yaw, and -- since the root can tilt via a\n play-space
      flip or a seat/vehicle -- pitch and roll too). Anything else means bulk body
      motion leaks\n into a joint DOF.\n\n Each invariance gate is paired with a
      Legacy_* gate that runs the old world-referenced formula and\n asserts it VIOLATES
      the invariant, so the numbers below are measured, not asserted into existence,\n
      and a revert to a world reference fails loudly instead of silently."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
