  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisSwivelHintConformanceTests
    MemberName: BasisSwivelHintConformanceTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: " <summary>\n Pins <see cref=\"BasisSwivelHintCore\"/> -- the LIVE RIG's
      feature construction for the swivel models --\n against the construction the
      models were actually FITTED in (BasisMocapAccuracy, which IS the fit\n pipeline).\n\n
      ================================================================================================\n
      WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND WHY IT WAS NOT ENOUGH.\n\n The mocap harness CANNOT
      verify a live-rig change. It drives the solve CORES directly; it never runs
      the\n animation job. So it can prove the model is good and still tell you nothing
      about whether the rig is\n feeding it correctly -- and a swivel model fed wrong
      does not degrade gracefully. It produces CONFIDENT\n GARBAGE, which is the
      only kind of wrong that survives a green suite.\n\n \u26A0 THIS FILE WAS GREEN
      WHILE THE ELBOWS WERE UP BY THE EARS IN A HEADSET. Twice over:\n\n   1. Every
      test here drove the model with a hand ON the arm (|tipLocal| <= 1), because
      that is all the\n      corpus contains. THE LIVE RIG IS HANDED THE RAW CONTROLLER
      TARGET, which sails past the avatar's\n      reach constantly -- and the model
      was a 3rd-order polynomial with NO DOMAIN CLAMP, i.e. a random\n      number
      generator out there. TheModel_RefusesToExtrapolate is the test that was missing.\n\n  
      2. The model read the hand's ROTATION, divided by a T-pose captured at job
      build -- but\n      BasisLocalAvatarDriver exits T-pose BEFORE it builds the
      rig, so that \"rest pose\" was not reliably a\n      rest pose. Those features
      are gone: a bone's rotation is a rig convention and it does not transfer.\n\n
      The lesson is written into the TESTS and not merely into the comments: every
      test here now drives the\n model with inputs THE LIVE RIG CAN ACTUALLY PRODUCE,
      especially the ones the corpus never contains.\n ================================================================================================\n
      </summary>"
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
