    Summary: "THE OVER-REACH PROBE -- the one thing the mocap corpus structurally
      CANNOT test.\n\n In mocap the hand/foot is always ON the limb, so |tipLocal|
      <= 1 on every frame the accuracy and\n motion-quality suites have ever seen.
      The LIVE RIG is handed the RAW controller target, which sails\n past the avatar's
      limb length whenever the user is bigger than their avatar -- i.e. body PROPORTION\n
      MISMATCH is exactly an over-reach of the input, and it happens constantly.
      Nothing driven by the BVH\n corpus can see it, because the corpus never goes
      there.\n\n This suite sweeps the tip from 0.3 to 1.6 limb-lengths along fixed
      directions and measures the worst\n single-step swivel change. A radial sweep
      holds the axis constant, so this is a clean bend rotation\n with no reference-frame
      artifact. A 'flip' is tens of degrees for a ~2-3 mm hand step.\n\n It pins
      two things the pole models must satisfy past reach, and which the polynomial's
      history shows\n are NOT free: (1) the swivel FREEZES radially past full extension
      -- pushing straight out further must\n not move the elbow/knee (the domain
      clamp guarantees it); (2) the model never FLIPS -- the unclamped\n polynomial
      is \"a random number generator\" outside |t|<=1 (its own words), so bounded,
      smooth\n behaviour there is a property to assert, not assume. The neural pole
      models are picked for accuracy\n AND for this smoothness (see tools/neural_ik/train_swivel.py),
      and measure smoother than the polynomial."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
