  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.BasisTrackerConfigMatrixTests
    MemberName: BasisTrackerConfigMatrixTests
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 1
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "WHAT DOES THE TRACKER COUNT ACTUALLY DO TO THE SPINE SOLVE?\n\n Basis
      runs 3-point through 11-point. The solver BRANCHES on how many of those points
      exist -- and\n until this file the branching was, in the harness sense, unmeasured.
      The two flags that carry it,\n `HasChestTracker` and `hasHipsTracker`, appear
      eleven times in runtime code and gate four separate\n pieces of behaviour,
      three of which change the pose in ways nothing else can reach:\n\n   BasisFullBodyIK.cs:406
      vs :429   TWO DIFFERENT SPINE PIPELINES.\n       chest tracker : chestDesired
      is double-clamped against neck AND spine by MaxChestDeltaProperty,\n                      
      written to the chest bone, then DistributeSpineBend -> BiasSpineTowardChest\n                      
      -> GuardSpineChain -> SolveSequentialSpineIK.\n       no chest      : DistributeSpineBend
      -> ApplyArmSwingChestFollow -> GuardSpineChain\n                       -> SolveSequentialSpineIK.\n  
      BasisFullBodyIK.cs:935           With a chest tracker the pre-bend's pitch
      AND roll weights are\n                                    ZEROED (SpineBendPitch/Roll,
      UpperBendPitch/Roll = 0), so\n                                    DistributeSpineBend
      does dramatically less work and the chain\n                                   
      below inherits a far straighter spine to bend.\n   BasisFullBodyIK.cs:1033         
      ApplyCrouchBodyOffset RETURNS EARLY AND DOES NOTHING AT ALL when\n                                   
      `HasChestTracker || hasHipsTracker`. A 6-point user's crouch is\n                                   
      not a tuned variant of a 3-point user's; it is a different code\n                                   
      path with no sit-back in it whatsoever.\n   BasisFullBodyIK.cs:381 / :395   
      ApplyTrunkCounterbalance and ApplyHipHinge are gated on the HIPS\n                                   
      flag alone -- deliberately narrower than the crouch gate, because\n                                   
      a chest tracker measures lean but the pelvis POSITION is still\n                                   
      synthesised.\n\n THE HOLE THIS CLOSES. BasisSpineCorpusAccuracyTests -- the
      accuracy ruler this suite trusts -- calls\n DistributeSpineBend and SolveSequentialSpineIK
      DIRECTLY. That is the right thing for measuring the\n spine chain, but it means
      it steps over the dispatch at :406 entirely, and therefore\n BiasSpineTowardChest,
      ApplyArmSwingChestFollow and GuardSpineChain were executed by NOTHING in the\n
      suite. This file drives the real public entry point, BasisFullIKConstraintJob.SolveSpine,
      so every\n one of those stages runs on real CMU mocap for every tracker configuration.\n\n
      HOW THE MATRIX IS KEPT HONEST. Four things, each of which was a way to accidentally
      measure nothing:\n\n   1. THE CHEST IS FED. `HasChestTracker = true` with no
      chest data is not an 8-point user, it is a\n      lie -- the chest branch would
      clamp and write a stale identity and the row would measure the\n      absence
      of data rather than the presence of a tracker. Every chest row is fed the subject's
      own\n      chest orientation, as a GEOMETRIC frame (chest->upperChest axis
      crossed with pelvis facing),\n      never the BVH's raw joint rotation, for
      the same reason GazeFrame is geometric: a bone's\n      rotation is a modelling
      convention and does not transfer, a segment's direction is anatomy and\n     
      does. Fed as a delta from the subject's own rest frame, because the built rig
      binds at identity.\n   2. THE HANDS ARE FED AND ENABLED. ApplyArmSwingChestFollow
      early-outs when both\n      `enabledLeftHand` and `enabledRightHand` are zero
      -- which is the default. A 3-point user has\n      two controllers, so leaving
      the hands off would have silently deleted the entire no-chest\n      branch's
      distinguishing stage and made the row identical to a pipeline nobody ships.\n  
      3. THE CROUCH IS FED. ApplyCrouchBodyOffset is inert while `standingHeadHeight`
      is zero (its\n      default), so the :1033 gate would have been invisible.
      Both scalars are packed exactly as\n      BasisLocalRigDriver packs them: standing
      height from the subject's most-upright frame, depth as\n      max(0, standing
      - live).\n   4. THE CHEST CONE IS SET PER MODE. MaxChestDeltaProperty is not
      only the chest tracker's clamp,\n      it is ALSO the CCD's chest cone (SolveSequentialSpineIK:522
      -> ClampChestCone:583), so it\n      shapes the solve with no chest tracker
      present at all. BasisSpineCorpusAccuracyTests never\n      sets it, which means
      its published figure was measured at a ZERO-degree chest cone while\n     
      production ships 90. The ruler here runs at 0 to reproduce the number it checks
      against and\n      the dispatch rows run at 90 to be the shipped solver; see
      the note in RunClip.\n   5. THE PELVIS TARGET IS GROUND TRUTH IN EVERY ROW,
      under LockHips -- but the pelvis the SOLVER\n      ENDS UP WITH IS NOT, and
      conflating the two is the single biggest trap in this file. Rows are\n     
      fed an identical, perfect hips target, so they differ only by the flags. What
      the flags then do\n      is decide whether that target survives: with a hips
      tracker it is written through untouched,\n      and without one SolveSpine
      SYNTHESISES a pelvis on top of it (:381 counterbalance, :386/:1033\n      crouch
      sit-back, :395 hinge). Measured on this corpus, the untracked rows' pelvis
      ends up\n      ~7.5 cm from the human on average and 37 cm at worst, while
      the tracked rows land on it exactly.\n      So \"identical inputs\" does NOT
      mean \"identical pelvis\", every joint in the table hangs off that\n      pelvis,
      and any comparison between a synthesising row and a planted one has to account
      for it.\n      TheTrackerMatrix does, via the matched floor described on its
      own summary; an earlier version of\n      this file did not, and consequently
      reported a 1.3 cm spine \"regression\" that was really a 7.5 cm\n      pelvis
      sitting underneath it.\n\n      READ THAT NUMBER CAREFULLY -- it is a DISPLACEMENT,
      not a residual. Because the row is fed a\n      PERFECT pelvis and the stages
      then move it, the metric measures how far the synthesis travels\n      from
      ground truth. The live 3-point rig is not fed a perfect pelvis: it gets the
      virtual spine's\n      head-derived estimate (BasisVirtualSpineCore), which
      the sit-back exists to CORRECT. So this\n      figure bounds the synthesis'
      authority, and is not by itself evidence the stages are wrong.\n      It is
      also concentrated: on frames where the subject is genuinely standing (trunk
      vertical,\n      knees straight, feet planted) the synthesised pelvis is 0.8
      cm from the human and only 0.2% of\n      those frames clear the crouch deadzone
      at all. The travel is a crouch behaviour, not a\n      standing-pose bias.\n\n
      THE RULER CROSS-CHECK IS THE POINT OF THE FILE. Before any matrix row is worth
      reading, the rig,\n the clip set, the gaze frame and the centimetre metric
      have to be shown to reproduce the number the\n shipped corpus test already
      publishes. TheRuler_ReproducesTheShippedCorpusFigure does exactly that\n and
      fails loudly if it does not. This project has repeatedly been burned by harnesses
      that measured\n a system the runtime never produces; a matrix built on an unverified
      ruler is fiction in a table.\n\n REPORT-ONLY on the matrix rows. There is no
      honest threshold for \"how accurate should an 8-point user\n be\" until the
      number exists, so the asserts hold only what is unambiguous: the spine solve
      beats\n freezing the spine AT THE SAME PELVIS (the one form of \"beats doing
      nothing\" that is fair to a row\n whose pelvis is invented), a planted-pelvis
      row additionally beats the ground-truth-pelvis floor,\n every error is finite,
      the pooled mean sits inside a sane absolute bound, the pelvis obeys its own\n
      gates, and the structural invariants hold (feet do not touch the spine dispatch;
      a hips tracker\n suppresses all three pelvis-synthesis stages exactly)."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
