  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.
    MemberName: TheTrackerMatrix_MeasuresEveryShippedConfiguration
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 1
    ParamNames:
    - LoadDispatchClips(
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "THE MATRIX. Solved-spine-vs-human error for every shipped tracker configuration,
      driven through\n the REAL dispatch (BasisFullIKConstraintJob.SolveSpine), so
      BiasSpineTowardChest,\n ApplyArmSwingChestFollow, GuardSpineChain, the pre-bend's
      chest-tracker weight zeroing and all\n three tracker-gated pelvis stages execute
      on real CMU mocap.\n\n ==========================================================================================\n
      WHY THERE ARE TWO FLOORS, AND WHY THE OBVIOUS ONE IS A TRAP.\n\n This test
      used to assert one thing of every row: \"the dispatch must beat the rigid rest
      pose.\"\n That comparison was NOT apples-to-apples, and the row it broke on
      was the most important one in\n the table.\n\n The RIGID floor plants the pelvis
      on the mocap ground truth (see the long note in RunClip -- it\n has to, or
      the floor absorbs the subject's whole walk across the capture volume). So rigid
      is\n handed a PERFECT pelvis for free. A dispatch row with no hips tracker
      is not: SolveSpine\n SYNTHESISES the pelvis for it, through ApplyTrunkCounterbalance
      (:381), ApplyCrouchBodyOffset\n (:386/:1033) and ApplyHipHinge (:395). All
      four measured joints hang off that pelvis, so the row\n carries the pelvis'
      error into every one of them while the floor carries none.\n\n MEASURED, on
      this exact clip set and stride, by replaying SolveSpine's pelvis arithmetic
      against\n the shipped cores offline -- the pelvis' own distance from the human:\n    
      3-point / 3-point +anatROM   7.5 cm mean, 30.1 cm p95, 37.3 cm worst\n    
      chest, no hips               3.5 cm mean,  8.7 cm p95,  9.3 cm worst  (crouch
      gated off by\n                                                                           
      the chest flag; only\n                                                                           
      the counterbalance runs)\n     every row WITH a hips tracker   0.00 cm -- the
      pelvis is the commanded target, bit for bit\n\n \"Moving on 99.9% of frames\"
      used to be quoted here too. It is true and it is misleading: it\n counts ANY
      displacement, and the trunk counterbalance has no deadzone, so it is nonzero
      on\n essentially every frame at a median of 0.6 cm while standing. The number
      worth quoting is how\n many frames clear the crouch DEADZONE and command a
      real sit-back: 58% on this ten-clip set,\n 37% across the full 109-clip corpus,
      and 0.2% on genuinely-standing frames.\n\n The gap the old assertion tripped
      on was 7.6 vs 6.3 cm, i.e. 1.3 cm. The pelvis under that row is\n nearly SIX
      TIMES that far from the human. Attributing 1.3 cm of joint error to \"the spine
      solve\n is worse than not solving\" while the pelvis beneath it is 7.5 cm out
      is not a measurement, it is a\n mislabelling -- and the version of this test
      that made it would have gone on failing for a reason\n that has nothing to
      do with the code it names.\n\n So each dispatch row is now measured against
      a MATCHED floor as well: the same pelvis the row\n itself produced, with the
      spine frozen at rest (RowData.FrozenAtSamePelvis). The pelvis error is\n then
      common to both sides and cancels, and what is left answers the question the
      assertion was\n always trying to ask -- given the pelvis this configuration
      produced, did bending the spine beat\n not bending it? For the hips-tracker
      rows the matched floor and the rigid floor are the same\n thing (their pelvis
      IS the ground truth), which is a useful self-check on the table.\n\n NOTHING
      HERE WAS WIDENED. The absolute bound is still 12 cm, the rigid-floor assertion
      is still\n enforced verbatim on every row it is honest for, and two assertions
      were ADDED (the matched floor,\n and the pelvis' own behaviour). The pelvis
      error is now its own printed column precisely so that\n it can never again
      hide inside a pooled spine figure -- in either direction.\n ==========================================================================================\n\n
      WHAT IT GUARDS, per row:\n   * the spine solve beats freezing the spine AT
      THE SAME PELVIS -- the isolated spine claim, and\n     the only \"solving beats
      not solving\" statement that is fair to every row,\n   * rows whose pelvis
      is planted additionally beat the ground-truth-pelvis rigid floor, which is\n    
      the original assertion kept wherever it is still apples-to-apples,\n   * the
      pelvis behaves as its gates promise: exactly planted with a hips tracker, actually
      moving\n     without one, and never beyond a smoke-alarm bound,\n   * every
      sample is finite -- a NaN anywhere in the dispatch would poison the live rig,\n  
      * the pooled mean stays inside a sane absolute bound of 12 cm, the same bound
      the corpus\n     accuracy test uses, which is loose enough to be a smoke alarm
      and not a tuning target.\n\n EVERY ROW IS MEASURED BEFORE ANY ROW FAILS. Failures
      are collected and reported together with the\n full table. The old loop asserted
      inside itself, so the first bad row aborted the run and the rest\n of the matrix
      was never measured at all -- which is how \"one configuration regressed\" and
      \"all\n three untracked-pelvis configurations regressed\" produced an identical-looking
      failure.\n\n The per-joint columns are printed because the chest rows are FED
      the chest: Chest, UpperChest and\n Neck are downstream of a measured orientation
      there and Spine is not, so a pooled figure alone\n would flatter the chest
      branch without saying why."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.Tests.IK.
    MemberName: TheChestBranch_DivergesFromTheNoChestBranch
    Kind: Method
    ParamCount: 1
    ParamNames:
    - LoadDispatchClips(
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "THE TWO SPINE PIPELINES ARE GENUINELY DIFFERENT PIPELINES.\n\n WHAT
      IT GUARDS: BasisFullBodyIK.cs:406 vs :429, and the pre-bend weight zeroing
      at :935. A chest\n tracker swaps ApplyArmSwingChestFollow for a clamped chest
      write plus BiasSpineTowardChest, and\n drops the pre-bend's pitch and roll
      to zero. If someone collapses those branches, or the chest\n feed silently
      stops arriving, the two configurations converge and this test is the alarm
      --\n without it the matrix above would keep printing two identical rows and
      look perfectly healthy.\n\n Asserted as a pure structural property (the poses
      differ by a measurable margin), never as a\n preference for one branch: which
      of the two is more accurate is what the matrix reports, and\n the answer is
      allowed to change. Headroom: the divergence is required to exceed 1 mm of mean\n
      joint displacement, which is far below any plausible real difference and far
      above float noise."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
