  - TypeFullName: Basis.MediaPipe.AvatarHandRig
    MemberName: AvatarHandRig
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Avatar hand geometry in player-root-local space. Correction maps a
      MediaPipe palm frame onto the\n hand bone's rotation; it is built from the
      knuckle positions, which do not move relative to the\n hand when the fingers
      curl, so it holds for any pose and needs no reference pose to capture.\n\n
      IkOffsetInverse undoes a SECOND palm->bone correction that the IK applies downstream.
      We hand our\n rotation to a tracker, and BasisArmSolveCore does `tRotation
      = TargetRotation * TargetOffset` with\n TargetOffset = data.m_CalibratedRotationLeft/RightHand,
      which BasisAnimationRiggingHelper builds as\n `Inverse(landmarkBind) * boneBind`
      -- i.e. it is ALSO a palm-frame -> hand-bone constant. That offset\n is correct
      for a producer that reports a PALM/LANDMARK frame (a real tracker does). But
      `Correction`\n above already finishes the job: we hand over a BONE rotation,
      so the IK's offset lands on top of it\n and the hand ends up wrong by exactly
      that offset -- and DIFFERENTLY ON EVERY AVATAR, because it is\n calibrated
      per rig. (The feet had this identical bug.)\n\n Pre-multiplying by its inverse
      makes the IK's own `* TargetOffset` collapse back to what we meant:\n     (boneRot
      * offset^-1) * offset == boneRot\n\n Note the two corrections are NOT the same
      quaternion, so this does not reduce to \"drop Correction\":\n MediaPipeSpace's
      palm frame uses the middle-MCP knuckle with a left-hand normal flip, while\n
      HandRotationFromLandmarks uses the index/pinky midpoint. `Correction * IkOffsetInverse`
      is exactly the\n residual between those two conventions. Deleting Correction
      instead would leave that residual behind\n as a silent frame error -- close
      enough to look plausible, which is worse than obviously wrong."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.MediaPipe.MediaPipeHeadConverter
